 two words.\nThe first word in an organism&#x27;s scientific name tells you the organism&#x27;s genus. A genus is a group of organisms that share many traits.\nA genus is made up of one or more species. A species is a group of very similar organisms. The second word in an organism&#x27;s scientific name tells you its species within its genus.\nTogether, the two parts of an organism&#x27;s scientific name identify its species. For example Ursus maritimus and Ursus americanus are two species of bears. They are part of the same genus, Ursus. But they are different species within the genus. Ursus maritimus has the species name maritimus. Ursus americanus has the species name americanus.\nBoth bears have small round ears and sharp claws. But Ursus maritimus has white fur and Ursus americanus has black fur. A Burmese python&#x27;s scientific name is Python bivittatus.\nMelanoplus bivittatus does have the same species within its genus as a Burmese python, but they are not in the same genus! They do not have the same scientific name as each other. So, these organisms are not in the same species.\nPython bivittatus has the same scientific name as a Burmese python. So, these organisms are in the same species.\nFalco peregrinus does not have the same scientific name as a Burmese python. So, Python bivittatus and Falco peregrinus are not in the same species.</p>\n          \n          <div class=\"hash-row\">\n            <span><strong>Source/License:</strong> ScienceQA Open Figure Benchmark</span>\n            <span><strong>SHA-256:</strong> <code>28c09c4f3633424e5acf...</code></span>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </details>\n    </article>\n    \n    <article class=\"q-card\">\n      <div class=\"q-head\">\n        <div class=\"q-badges\">\n          <span class=\"badge b-id\">#55 · SUBMEDENT_Q493470</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-db\">DB1_SCRAPED_MODIFIED</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-psg\">📖 Passage PSG_FB47C3A2F2BF (Question 5 of 5)</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-fig\">🖼️ Figure / Diagram</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-loc\">UNIVERSAL: ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-subj\">SUB_MED_ENT — Otorhinolaryngology (ENT)</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-diff medium\">Medium</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-pyq\">PYQ: Standard Science Curriculum Figure Assessment (2023)</span><span class=\"badge b-meta\">Body: Open Science Curriculum · ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"q-meta\">\n          <span><strong>Domain:</strong> Medical &amp; Health Sciences</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Unit:</strong> Classification and scientific names</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Topic:</strong> biology</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      \n        <div class=\"passage-box\">\n          <div class=\"passage-hdr\">\n            <span>📖 Context Passage — Biology</span>\n            <span class=\"passage-tag\">Shared ID: PSG_FB47C3A2F2BF · Question 5 of 5</span>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"passage-body\"><p>This organism is a Burmese python. Its scientific name is Python bivittatus.</p></div>\n          \n        </div>\n        \n      <div class=\"q-stem\"><p>Select the organism in the same genus as the Burmese python.</p></div>\n      <div class=\"q-diagram-box\"><img class=\"q-figure-img\" src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Official Exam Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n      <div class=\"opts-grid\"><div class=\"opt-item correct-opt\"><span class=\"opt-letter\">A</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Python reticulatus</div><span class=\"corr-tag\">✓ Correct</span></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">B</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Chroicocephalus novaehollandiae</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">C</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Melanoplus bivittatus</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">D</span><div class=\"opt-text\">None of the above</div></div></div>\n      <details class=\"expl-box\">\n        <summary>View Explanation & Provenance (Answer: Option A)</summary>\n        <div class=\"expl-body\">\n          <p>Scientists use scientific names to identify organisms. Scientific names are made of two words.\nThe first word in an organism&#x27;s scientific name tells you the organism&#x27;s genus. A genus is a group of organisms that share many traits.\nA genus is made up of one or more species. A species is a group of very similar organisms. The second word in an organism&#x27;s scientific name tells you its species within its genus.\nTogether, the two parts of an organism&#x27;s scientific name identify its species. For example Ursus maritimus and Ursus americanus are two species of bears. They are part of the same genus, Ursus. But they are different species within the genus. Ursus maritimus has the species name maritimus. Ursus americanus has the species name americanus.\nBoth bears have small round ears and sharp claws. But Ursus maritimus has white fur and Ursus americanus has black fur. A Burmese python&#x27;s scientific name is Python bivittatus. The first word of its scientific name is Python.\nPython reticulatus is in the genus Python. The first word of its scientific name is Python. So, Python reticulatus and Python bivittatus are in the same genus.\nMelanoplus bivittatus and Python bivittatus are not in the same genus.\nThese organisms are not in the same genus, but part of their scientific names is the same. Melanoplus bivittatus and Python bivittatus have the same species name within their genus, bivittatus. But the first words of their scientific names are different. Melanoplus bivittatus is in the genus Melanoplus, and Python bivittatus is in the genus Python.\nChroicocephalus novaehollandiae is in the genus Chroicocephalus. The first word of its scientific name is Chroicocephalus. So, Chroicocephalus novaehollandiae and Python bivittatus are not in the same genus.</p>\n          \n          <div class=\"hash-row\">\n            <span><strong>Source/License:</strong> ScienceQA Open Figure Benchmark</span>\n            <span><strong>SHA-256:</strong> <code>a9340522bb79ee77fd49...</code></span>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </details>\n    </article>\n    \n    <article class=\"q-card\">\n      <div class=\"q-head\">\n        <div class=\"q-badges\">\n          <span class=\"badge b-id\">#56 · SUBMEDENT_Q495350</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-db\">DB1_SCRAPED_MODIFIED</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-psg\">📖 Passage PSG_FB2022F53A84 (Question 1 of 1)</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-loc\">UNIVERSAL: ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-subj\">SUB_MED_ENT — Otorhinolaryngology (ENT)</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-diff medium\">Medium</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-pyq\">PYQ: Standard Science Curriculum Figure Assessment (2023)</span><span class=\"badge b-meta\">Body: Open Science Curriculum · ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"q-meta\">\n          <span><strong>Domain:</strong> Medical &amp; Health Sciences</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Unit:</strong> Designing experiments</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Topic:</strong> science-and-engineering-practices</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      \n        <div class=\"passage-box\">\n          <div class=\"passage-hdr\">\n            <span>📖 Context Passage — Science And Engineering Practices</span>\n            <span class=\"passage-tag\">Shared ID: PSG_FB2022F53A84 · Question 1 of 1</span>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"passage-body\"><p>After Patrick cleans up a spill, he hangs a wet cloth towel in the laundry room. Two hours later, he notices that the towel has partially dried. He wonders what factors affect how cloth dries. So, he decides to design an experiment. He has the following supplies available:\ntwo identical white cloth towels\nwater\na clothesline in the laundry room\na clothesline in the backyard</p></div>\n          \n        </div>\n        \n      <div class=\"q-stem\"><p>Using only these supplies, which question can Patrick investigate with an experiment?</p></div>\n      \n      <div class=\"opts-grid\"><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">A</span><div class=\"opt-text\">When hung in the laundry room, do black cloth towels or white cloth towels dry more quickly?</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">B</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Does a small cloth towel or a large cloth towel dry faster when hung in the backyard?</div></div><div class=\"opt-item correct-opt\"><span class=\"opt-letter\">C</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Do cloth towels dry faster if they are hung in the laundry room or in the backyard?</div><span class=\"corr-tag\">✓ Correct</span></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">D</span><div class=\"opt-text\">None of the above</div></div></div>\n      <details class=\"expl-box\">\n        <summary>View Explanation & Provenance (Answer: Option C)</summary>\n        <div class=\"expl-body\">\n          <p>Experiments can be designed to answer specific questions. When designing an experiment, you must identify the supplies that are necessary to answer your question. In order to do this, you need to figure out what will be tested and what will be measured during the experiment.\nImagine that you are wondering if plants grow to different heights when planted in different types of soil. How might you decide what supplies are necessary to conduct this experiment?\nFirst, you need to identify the part of the experiment that will be tested, which is the independent variable. This is usually the part of the experiment that is different or changed. In this case, you would like to know how plants grow in different types of soil. So, you must have different types of soil available.\nNext, you need to identify the part of the experiment that will be measured or observed, which is the dependent variable. In this experiment, you would like to know if some plants grow taller than others. So, you must be able to compare the plants&#x27; heights. To do this, you can observe which plants are taller by looking at them, or you can measure their exact heights with a meterstick.\nSo, if you have different types of soil and can observe or measure the heights of your plants, then you have the supplies you need to investigate your question with an experiment!</p>\n          \n          <div class=\"hash-row\">\n            <span><strong>Source/License:</strong> ScienceQA Open Figure Benchmark</span>\n            <span><strong>SHA-256:</strong> <code>79fdb49bc4304a3ee74c...</code></span>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </details>\n    </article>\n    \n    <article class=\"q-card\">\n      <div class=\"q-head\">\n        <div class=\"q-badges\">\n          <span class=\"badge b-id\">#57 · SUBGSGK_Q713945</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-db\">DB1_SCRAPED_MODIFIED</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-psg\">📖 Passage PSG_FAF2E20CFD99 (Question 1 of 2)</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-loc\">UNIVERSAL: ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-subj\">SUB_GS_GK — Multilingual Reading Comprehension &amp; Analytical Verbal Ability</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-diff medium\">Medium</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-pyq\">PYQ: Multilingual Standardized Reading Comprehension (acm_Arab) (2023-2024)</span><span class=\"badge b-meta\">Body: Open Multilingual Comprehension Benchmark (CC-BY-SA-4.0) · ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"q-meta\">\n          <span><strong>Domain:</strong> Aptitude &amp; Comprehension</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Unit:</strong> Shared-Passage Comprehension (acm_Arab)</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Topic:</strong> Multi-Question Reading Comprehension (ACM)</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      \n        <div class=\"passage-box\">\n          <div class=\"passage-hdr\">\n            <span>📖 Shared Comprehension Passage (acm_Arab)</span>\n            <span class=\"passage-tag\">Shared ID: PSG_FAF2E20CFD99 · Question 1 of 2</span>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"passage-body\"><p>وراح تشير الوثيقة، بحسب التسريب، للخلاف على الحدود، اللي تريدها فلسطين مبنية على حدود ما قبل حرب عام 1967 بالشرق الأوسط. الموضوعات الأخرى اللي يُقال أنها تمت تغطيتها تضمن الحالة المستقبلية للقدس واللي تعتبر قضية مهمة للشعبين ومشكلة وادي الأردن. تطالب إسرائيل باستمرار التواجد العسكري بالغور لمدة عشر سنوات، بمجرد توقيع اتفاق، بينما توافق السلطة الفلسطينية على استمرار هذا التواجد لخمس سنوات فقط.</p></div>\n          \n        </div>\n        \n      <div class=\"q-stem\"><p>بحسب الفقرة أي من المشاكل التالية ما احتوتها المذكرة؟</p></div>\n      \n      <div class=\"opts-grid\"><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">A</span><div class=\"opt-text\">مستقبل القدس</div></div><div class=\"opt-item correct-opt\"><span class=\"opt-letter\">B</span><div class=\"opt-text\">سبب حدوث حرب 1967</div><span class=\"corr-tag\">✓ Correct</span></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">C</span><div class=\"opt-text\">وجود الجيش بالوادي</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">D</span><div class=\"opt-text\">الخلاف على الحدود</div></div></div>\n      <details class=\"expl-box\">\n        <summary>View Explanation & Provenance (Answer: Option B)</summary>\n        <div class=\"expl-body\">\n          <p>The passage mentions the conflict over borders, which is based on the borders before the 1967 war in the Middle East. It also discusses the future status of Jerusalem, which is an important issue for both peoples, and the problem of the Jordan Valley. Israel consistently demands a ten-year military presence in the Jordan Valley, while the Palestinian Authority agrees to a five-year continuation of this presence. The passage does not mention the cause of the 1967 war, so option B is correct. The other options are mentioned in the passage, making them incorrect.</p>\n          \n          <div class=\"hash-row\">\n            <span><strong>Source/License:</strong> Belebele CC-BY-SA-4.0 Multilingual Shared-Passage Bank (acm_Arab) · AI_AUDITED_VERIFIED (g4f:keyless)</span>\n            <span><strong>SHA-256:</strong> <code>4003f0816c6d9bafb504...</code></span>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </details>\n    </article>\n    \n    <article class=\"q-card\">\n      <div class=\"q-head\">\n        <div class=\"q-badges\">\n          <span class=\"badge b-id\">#58 · SUBGSGK_Q713946</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-db\">DB1_SCRAPED_MODIFIED</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-psg\">📖 Passage PSG_FAF2E20CFD99 (Question 2 of 2)</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-loc\">UNIVERSAL: ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-subj\">SUB_GS_GK — Multilingual Reading Comprehension &amp; Analytical Verbal Ability</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-diff medium\">Medium</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-pyq\">PYQ: Multilingual Standardized Reading Comprehension (acm_Arab) (2023-2024)</span><span class=\"badge b-meta\">Body: Open Multilingual Comprehension Benchmark (CC-BY-SA-4.0) · ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"q-meta\">\n          <span><strong>Domain:</strong> Aptitude &amp; Comprehension</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Unit:</strong> Shared-Passage Comprehension (acm_Arab)</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Topic:</strong> Multi-Question Reading Comprehension (ACM)</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      \n        <div class=\"passage-box\">\n          <div class=\"passage-hdr\">\n            <span>📖 Shared Comprehension Passage (acm_Arab)</span>\n            <span class=\"passage-tag\">Shared ID: PSG_FAF2E20CFD99 · Question 2 of 2</span>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"passage-body\"><p>وراح تشير الوثيقة، بحسب التسريب، للخلاف على الحدود، اللي تريدها فلسطين مبنية على حدود ما قبل حرب عام 1967 بالشرق الأوسط. الموضوعات الأخرى اللي يُقال أنها تمت تغطيتها تضمن الحالة المستقبلية للقدس واللي تعتبر قضية مهمة للشعبين ومشكلة وادي الأردن. تطالب إسرائيل باستمرار التواجد العسكري بالغور لمدة عشر سنوات، بمجرد توقيع اتفاق، بينما توافق السلطة الفلسطينية على استمرار هذا التواجد لخمس سنوات فقط.</p></div>\n          \n        </div>\n        \n      <div class=\"q-stem\"><p>أي من التالي مو من مطالب فلسطين؟</p></div>\n      \n      <div class=\"opts-grid\"><div class=\"opt-item correct-opt\"><span class=\"opt-letter\">A</span><div class=\"opt-text\">الحدود بحسب شروط ما قبل الحرب</div><span class=\"corr-tag\">✓ Correct</span></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">B</span><div class=\"opt-text\">وجود الجيش بالوادي الأردني مستمر</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">C</span><div class=\"opt-text\">الإجابات بخصوص مستقبل القدس</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">D</span><div class=\"opt-text\">وجود الجيش لخمس سنوات بالوادي الأردني</div></div></div>\n      <details class=\"expl-box\">\n        <summary>View Explanation & Provenance (Answer: Option A)</summary>\n        <div class=\"expl-body\">\n          <p>According to the passage, the Palestinian demand is for the borders to be based on the conditions before the war. The passage does not mention the military presence in the Jordan Valley or the future status of Jerusalem, so options B, C, and D are incorrect. Option A is correct as it directly refers to the passage.</p>\n          \n          <div class=\"hash-row\">\n            <span><strong>Source/License:</strong> Belebele CC-BY-SA-4.0 Multilingual Shared-Passage Bank (acm_Arab) · AI_AUDITED_VERIFIED (g4f:keyless)</span>\n            <span><strong>SHA-256:</strong> <code>5ac356853b6facf74872...</code></span>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </details>\n    </article>\n    \n    <article class=\"q-card\">\n      <div class=\"q-head\">\n        <div class=\"q-badges\">\n          <span class=\"badge b-id\">#59 · SUBSCIPHYS_Q495338</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-db\">DB1_SCRAPED_MODIFIED</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-psg\">📖 Passage PSG_FAE7C67EA8E0 (Question 1 of 2)</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-fig\">🖼️ Figure / Diagram</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-loc\">UNIVERSAL: ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-subj\">SUB_SCI_PHYSICS — General Science (Physics, Chemistry, Biology &amp; Geography)</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-diff medium\">Medium</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-pyq\">PYQ: Standard Science Curriculum Figure Assessment (2023)</span><span class=\"badge b-meta\">Body: Open Science Curriculum · ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"q-meta\">\n          <span><strong>Domain:</strong> Science</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Unit:</strong> Weather and climate</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Topic:</strong> earth-science</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      \n        <div class=\"passage-box\">\n          <div class=\"passage-hdr\">\n            <span>📖 Context Passage — Earth Science</span>\n            <span class=\"passage-tag\">Shared ID: PSG_FAE7C67EA8E0 · Question 1 of 2</span>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"passage-body\"><p>The map below shows humidity in the lower atmosphere on March 19, 2017. The map shows specific humidity, a measurement of the amount of water vapor in the air. The outlined area shows an air mass that influenced weather in Asia on that day.\nLook at the map. Then, answer the question below.\nData source: United States National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration/Earth System Research Laboratory, Physical Sciences Division</p></div>\n          \n        </div>\n        \n      <div class=\"q-stem\"><p>Which specific humidity level was measured within the outlined area shown?</p></div>\n      <div class=\"q-diagram-box\"><img class=\"q-figure-img\" src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Official Exam Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n      <div class=\"opts-grid\"><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">A</span><div class=\"opt-text\">10 grams of water vapor per kilogram of air</div></div><div class=\"opt-item correct-opt\"><span class=\"opt-letter\">B</span><div class=\"opt-text\">1 grams of water vapor per kilogram of air</div><span class=\"corr-tag\">✓ Correct</span></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">C</span><div class=\"opt-text\">12 grams of water vapor per kilogram of air</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">D</span><div class=\"opt-text\">None of the above</div></div></div>\n      <details class=\"expl-box\">\n        <summary>View Explanation & Provenance (Answer: Option B)</summary>\n        <div class=\"expl-body\">\n          <p>To study air masses, scientists can use maps that show conditions within Earth&#x27;s atmosphere. For example, the map below uses color to show specific humidity, a measurement of the amount of water vapor in the air.\nThe map&#x27;s legend tells you the specific humidity level that each color represents. Colors on the left in the legend represent lower specific humidity levels than colors on the right. For example, areas on the map that are the darkest shade of purple have a specific humidity from zero grams per kilogram (g/kg) up to two g/kg. Areas that are the next darkest shade of purple have a specific humidity from two g/kg up to four g/kg. Look at the colors shown within the outlined area. Then, use the legend to determine which specific humidity levels those colors represent.\nThe legend tells you that this air mass contained air with specific humidity levels between 0 and 4 grams of water vapor per kilogram of air.\n1 grams of water vapor per kilogram of air is within this range.\n10 and 12 grams of water vapor per kilogram of air are outside of this range.</p>\n          \n          <div class=\"hash-row\">\n            <span><strong>Source/License:</strong> ScienceQA Open Figure Benchmark</span>\n            <span><strong>SHA-256:</strong> <code>8538efbb02c6ec1ea8e3...</code></span>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </details>\n    </article>\n    \n    <article class=\"q-card\">\n      <div class=\"q-head\">\n        <div class=\"q-badges\">\n          <span class=\"badge b-id\">#60 · SUBSCIPHYS_Q495428</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-db\">DB1_SCRAPED_MODIFIED</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-psg\">📖 Passage PSG_FAE7C67EA8E0 (Question 2 of 2)</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-fig\">🖼️ Figure / Diagram</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-loc\">UNIVERSAL: ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-subj\">SUB_SCI_PHYSICS — General Science (Physics, Chemistry, Biology &amp; Geography)</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-diff medium\">Medium</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-pyq\">PYQ: Standard Science Curriculum Figure Assessment (2023)</span><span class=\"badge b-meta\">Body: Open Science Curriculum · ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"q-meta\">\n          <span><strong>Domain:</strong> Science</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Unit:</strong> Weather and climate</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Topic:</strong> earth-science</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      \n        <div class=\"passage-box\">\n          <div class=\"passage-hdr\">\n            <span>📖 Context Passage — Earth Science</span>\n            <span class=\"passage-tag\">Shared ID: PSG_FAE7C67EA8E0 · Question 2 of 2</span>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"passage-body\"><p>The map below shows humidity in the lower atmosphere on March 19, 2017. The map shows specific humidity, a measurement of the amount of water vapor in the air. The outlined area shows an air mass that influenced weather in Asia on that day.\nLook at the map. Then, answer the question below.\nData source: United States National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration/Earth System Research Laboratory, Physical Sciences Division</p></div>\n          \n        </div>\n        \n      <div class=\"q-stem\"><p>Which specific humidity level was measured within the outlined area shown?</p></div>\n      <div class=\"q-diagram-box\"><img class=\"q-figure-img\" src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Official Exam Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n      <div class=\"opts-grid\"><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">A</span><div class=\"opt-text\">9 grams of water vapor per kilogram of air</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">B</span><div class=\"opt-text\">12 grams of water vapor per kilogram of air</div></div><div class=\"opt-item correct-opt\"><span class=\"opt-letter\">C</span><div class=\"opt-text\">3 grams of water vapor per kilogram of air</div><span class=\"corr-tag\">✓ Correct</span></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">D</span><div class=\"opt-text\">None of the above</div></div></div>\n      <details class=\"expl-box\">\n        <summary>View Explanation & Provenance (Answer: Option C)</summary>\n        <div class=\"expl-body\">\n          <p>To study air masses, scientists can use maps that show conditions within Earth&#x27;s atmosphere. For example, the map below uses color to show specific humidity, a measurement of the amount of water vapor in the air.\nThe map&#x27;s legend tells you the specific humidity level that each color represents. Colors on the left in the legend represent lower specific humidity levels than colors on the right. For example, areas on the map that are the darkest shade of purple have a specific humidity from zero grams per kilogram (g/kg) up to two g/kg. Areas that are the next darkest shade of purple have a specific humidity from two g/kg up to four g/kg. Look at the colors shown within the outlined area. Then, use the legend to determine which specific humidity levels those colors represent.\nThe legend tells you that this air mass contained air with specific humidity levels between 0 and 4 grams of water vapor per kilogram of air.\n3 grams of water vapor per kilogram of air is within this range.\n9 and 12 grams of water vapor per kilogram of air are outside of this range.</p>\n          \n          <div class=\"hash-row\">\n            <span><strong>Source/License:</strong> ScienceQA Open Figure Benchmark</span>\n            <span><strong>SHA-256:</strong> <code>49577075ecb7c27404fb...</code></span>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </details>\n    </article>\n    \n    <article class=\"q-card\">\n      <div class=\"q-head\">\n        <div class=\"q-badges\">\n          <span class=\"badge b-id\">#61 · SUBMEENGME_Q493168</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-db\">DB1_SCRAPED_MODIFIED</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-psg\">📖 Passage PSG_FADF14065B7A (Question 1 of 1)</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-fig\">🖼️ Figure / Diagram</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-loc\">UNIVERSAL: ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-subj\">SUB_ME_ENG_MECH — Engineering Mechanics &amp; Strength of Materials</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-diff medium\">Medium</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-pyq\">PYQ: Standard Science Curriculum Figure Assessment (2023)</span><span class=\"badge b-meta\">Body: Open Science Curriculum · ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"q-meta\">\n          <span><strong>Domain:</strong> Engineering</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Unit:</strong> Engineering practices</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Topic:</strong> science-and-engineering-practices</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      \n        <div class=\"passage-box\">\n          <div class=\"passage-hdr\">\n            <span>📖 Context Passage — Science And Engineering Practices</span>\n            <span class=\"passage-tag\">Shared ID: PSG_FADF14065B7A · Question 1 of 1</span>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"passage-body\"><p>People can use the engineering-design process to develop solutions to problems. One step in the process is testing if a potential solution meets the requirements of the design.\nThe passage below describes how the engineering-design process was used to test a solution to a problem. Read the passage. Then answer the question below.\n\nFernando was . At the plant, an expensive filter was used to remove disease-causing bacteria from the water. But over time, the filter would become clogged with bacteria. If the filter became clogged, the water would not move through quickly enough. Fernando had to decide when the filter was too clogged and needed to be replaced. So, during his inspection, Fernando checked the filter by measuring how quickly water moved through it.\nFigure: an engineer at a water treatment plant.</p></div>\n          \n        </div>\n        \n      <div class=\"q-stem\"><p>Which of the following could Fernando&#x27;s test show?</p></div>\n      <div class=\"q-diagram-box\"><img class=\"q-figure-img\" src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Official Exam Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n      <div class=\"opts-grid\"><div class=\"opt-item correct-opt\"><span class=\"opt-letter\">A</span><div class=\"opt-text\">whether the filter was clogged</div><span class=\"corr-tag\">✓ Correct</span></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">B</span><div class=\"opt-text\">the amount of bacteria in the water before it was filtered</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">C</span><div class=\"opt-text\">whether an inexpensive filter would become clogged more often</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">D</span><div class=\"opt-text\">None of the above</div></div></div>\n      <details class=\"expl-box\">\n        <summary>View Explanation & Provenance (Answer: Option A)</summary>\n        <div class=\"expl-body\">\n          <p>People can use the engineering-design process to develop solutions to problems. One step in the process is testing if a potential solution meets the requirements of the design. How can you determine what a test can show? You need to figure out what was tested and what was measured.\nImagine an engineer needs to design a bridge for a windy location. She wants to make sure the bridge will not move too much in high wind. So, she builds a smaller prototype, or model, of a bridge. Then, she exposes the prototype to high winds and measures how much the bridge moves.\nFirst, identify what was tested. A test can examine one design, or it may compare multiple prototypes to each other. In the test described above, the engineer tested a prototype of a bridge in high wind.\nThen, identify what the test measured. One of the criteria for the bridge was that it not move too much in high winds. The test measured how much the prototype bridge moved.\nTests can show how well one or more designs meet the criteria. The test described above can show whether the bridge would move too much in high winds.</p>\n          \n          <div class=\"hash-row\">\n            <span><strong>Source/License:</strong> ScienceQA Open Figure Benchmark</span>\n            <span><strong>SHA-256:</strong> <code>2314e98988c642dcb99b...</code></span>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </details>\n    </article>\n    \n    <article class=\"q-card\">\n      <div class=\"q-head\">\n        <div class=\"q-badges\">\n          <span class=\"badge b-id\">#62 · SUBSCIBIOL_Q490440</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-db\">DB1_SCRAPED_MODIFIED</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-psg\">📖 Passage PSG_FA55560966EC (Question 1 of 2)</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-fig\">🖼️ Figure / Diagram</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-loc\">UNIVERSAL: ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-subj\">SUB_SCI_BIOLOGY — Life Sciences &amp; Biology (Botany, Zoology, Cell Biology, Genetics &amp; Ecology)</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-diff medium\">Medium</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-pyq\">PYQ: Standard Science Curriculum Figure Assessment (2023)</span><span class=\"badge b-meta\">Body: Open Science Curriculum · ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"q-meta\">\n          <span><strong>Domain:</strong> Science</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Unit:</strong> Genes to traits</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Topic:</strong> biology</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      \n        <div class=\"passage-box\">\n          <div class=\"passage-hdr\">\n            <span>📖 Context Passage — Biology</span>\n            <span class=\"passage-tag\">Shared ID: PSG_FA55560966EC · Question 1 of 2</span>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"passage-body\"><p>In a group of Labrador retrievers, some individuals have black fur and others have brown fur. In this group, the gene for the fur color trait has two alleles. The allele for black fur (F) is dominant over the allele for brown fur (f).\nThis Punnett square shows a cross between two Labrador retrievers.</p></div>\n          \n        </div>\n        \n      <div class=\"q-stem\"><p>What is the probability that a Labrador retriever produced by this cross will have brown fur?</p></div>\n      <div class=\"q-diagram-box\"><img class=\"q-figure-img\" src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Official Exam Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n      <div class=\"opts-grid\"><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">A</span><div class=\"opt-text\">4/4</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">B</span><div class=\"opt-text\">2/4</div></div><div class=\"opt-item correct-opt\"><span class=\"opt-letter\">C</span><div class=\"opt-text\">0/4</div><span class=\"corr-tag\">✓ Correct</span></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">D</span><div class=\"opt-text\">3/4</div></div></div>\n      <details class=\"expl-box\">\n        <summary>View Explanation & Provenance (Answer: Option C)</summary>\n        <div class=\"expl-body\">\n          <p>Offspring phenotypes: dominant or recessive?\nHow do you determine an organism&#x27;s phenotype for a trait? Look at the combination of alleles in the organism&#x27;s genotype for the gene that affects that trait. Some alleles have types called dominant and recessive. These two types can cause different versions of the trait to appear as the organism&#x27;s phenotype.\nIf an organism&#x27;s genotype has at least one dominant allele for a gene, the organism&#x27;s phenotype will be the dominant allele&#x27;s version of the gene&#x27;s trait.\nIf an organism&#x27;s genotype has only recessive alleles for a gene, the organism&#x27;s phenotype will be the recessive allele&#x27;s version of the gene&#x27;s trait.\nIn a Punnett square, each box represents a different outcome, or result. Each of the four outcomes is equally likely to happen. Each box represents one way the parents&#x27; alleles can combine to form an offspring&#x27;s genotype. Because there are four boxes in the Punnett square, there are four possible outcomes.\nAn event is a set of one or more outcomes. The probability of an event is a measure of how likely the event is to happen. This probability is a number between 0 and 1, and it can be written as a fraction:\nprobability of an event = number of ways the event can happen / number of equally likely outcomes\nYou can use a Punnett square to calculate the probability that a cross will produce certain offspring. For example, the Punnett square below has two boxes with the genotype Ff. It has one box with the genotype FF and one box with the genotype ff. This means there are two ways the parents&#x27; alleles can combine to form Ff. There is one way they can combine to form FF and one way they can combine to form ff.\n | F | f\nF | FF | Ff\nf | Ff | ff\nConsider an event in which this cross produces an offspring with the genotype ff. The probability of this event is given by the following fraction:\nnumber of ways the event can happen / number of equally likely outcomes = number of boxes with the genotype ff / total number of boxes = 1 / 4.</p>\n          \n          <div class=\"hash-row\">\n            <span><strong>Source/License:</strong> ScienceQA Open Figure Benchmark</span>\n            <span><strong>SHA-256:</strong> <code>8878b9d14497140c89a8...</code></span>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </details>\n    </article>\n    \n    <article class=\"q-card\">\n      <div class=\"q-head\">\n        <div class=\"q-badges\">\n          <span class=\"badge b-id\">#63 · SUBSCIBIOL_Q491178</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-db\">DB1_SCRAPED_MODIFIED</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-psg\">📖 Passage PSG_FA55560966EC (Question 2 of 2)</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-fig\">🖼️ Figure / Diagram</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-loc\">UNIVERSAL: ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-subj\">SUB_SCI_BIOLOGY — Life Sciences &amp; Biology (Botany, Zoology, Cell Biology, Genetics &amp; Ecology)</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-diff medium\">Medium</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-pyq\">PYQ: Standard Science Curriculum Figure Assessment (2023)</span><span class=\"badge b-meta\">Body: Open Science Curriculum · ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"q-meta\">\n          <span><strong>Domain:</strong> Science</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Unit:</strong> Genes to traits</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Topic:</strong> biology</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      \n        <div class=\"passage-box\">\n          <div class=\"passage-hdr\">\n            <span>📖 Context Passage — Biology</span>\n            <span class=\"passage-tag\">Shared ID: PSG_FA55560966EC · Question 2 of 2</span>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"passage-body\"><p>In a group of Labrador retrievers, some individuals have black fur and others have brown fur. In this group, the gene for the fur color trait has two alleles. The allele for black fur (F) is dominant over the allele for brown fur (f).\nThis Punnett square shows a cross between two Labrador retrievers.</p></div>\n          \n        </div>\n        \n      <div class=\"q-stem\"><p>What is the probability that a Labrador retriever produced by this cross will be heterozygous for the fur color gene?</p></div>\n      <div class=\"q-diagram-box\"><img class=\"q-figure-img\" src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Official Exam Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n      <div class=\"opts-grid\"><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">A</span><div class=\"opt-text\">3/4</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">B</span><div class=\"opt-text\">2/4</div></div><div class=\"opt-item correct-opt\"><span class=\"opt-letter\">C</span><div class=\"opt-text\">4/4</div><span class=\"corr-tag\">✓ Correct</span></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">D</span><div class=\"opt-text\">0/4</div></div></div>\n      <details class=\"expl-box\">\n        <summary>View Explanation & Provenance (Answer: Option C)</summary>\n        <div class=\"expl-body\">\n          <p>Offspring genotypes: homozygous or heterozygous?\nHow do you determine whether an organism is homozygous or heterozygous for a gene? Look at the alleles in the organism&#x27;s genotype for that gene.\nAn organism with two identical alleles for a gene is homozygous for that gene.\nIf both alleles are dominant, the organism is homozygous dominant for the gene.\nIf both alleles are recessive, the organism is homozygous recessive for the gene.\nAn organism with two different alleles for a gene is heterozygous for that gene.\nIn a Punnett square, each box represents a different outcome, or result. Each of the four outcomes is equally likely to happen. Each box represents one way the parents&#x27; alleles can combine to form an offspring&#x27;s genotype. \nBecause there are four boxes in the Punnett square, there are four possible outcomes.\nAn event is a set of one or more outcomes. The probability of an event is a measure of how likely the event is to happen. This probability is a number between 0 and 1, and it can be written as a fraction:\nprobability of an event = number of ways the event can happen / number of equally likely outcomes\nYou can use a Punnett square to calculate the probability that a cross will produce certain offspring. For example, the Punnett square below has two boxes with the genotype Ff. It has one box with the genotype FF and one box with the genotype ff. This means there are two ways the parents&#x27; alleles can combine to form Ff. There is one way they can combine to form FF and one way they can combine to form ff.\n | F | f\nF | FF | Ff\nf | Ff | ff\nConsider an event in which this cross produces an offspring with the genotype ff. The probability of this event is given by the following fraction:\nnumber of ways the event can happen / number of equally likely outcomes = number of boxes with the genotype ff / total number of boxes = 1 / 4</p>\n          \n          <div class=\"hash-row\">\n            <span><strong>Source/License:</strong> ScienceQA Open Figure Benchmark</span>\n            <span><strong>SHA-256:</strong> <code>c04d7b969b40e91e1dee...</code></span>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </details>\n    </article>\n    \n    <article class=\"q-card\">\n      <div class=\"q-head\">\n        <div class=\"q-badges\">\n          <span class=\"badge b-id\">#64 · SUBMEENGME_Q491389</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-db\">DB1_SCRAPED_MODIFIED</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-psg\">📖 Passage PSG_FA39DF91036E (Question 1 of 1)</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-fig\">🖼️ Figure / Diagram</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-loc\">UNIVERSAL: ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-subj\">SUB_ME_ENG_MECH — Engineering Mechanics &amp; Strength of Materials</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-diff medium\">Medium</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-pyq\">PYQ: Standard Science Curriculum Figure Assessment (2023)</span><span class=\"badge b-meta\">Body: Open Science Curriculum · ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"q-meta\">\n          <span><strong>Domain:</strong> Engineering</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Unit:</strong> Engineering practices</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Topic:</strong> science-and-engineering-practices</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      \n        <div class=\"passage-box\">\n          <div class=\"passage-hdr\">\n            <span>📖 Context Passage — Science And Engineering Practices</span>\n            <span class=\"passage-tag\">Shared ID: PSG_FA39DF91036E · Question 1 of 1</span>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"passage-body\"><p>People can use the engineering-design process to develop solutions to problems. One step in the process is testing if a potential solution meets the requirements of the design.\nThe passage below describes how the engineering-design process was used to test a solution to a problem. Read the passage. Then answer the question below.\n\nKeith was installing solar panels on the roof of a client&#x27;s house. The panels had to provide enough electricity to power the house year-round. Keith needed to decide how many panels to install and which side of the roof to install them on. If he put the panels on the side that got the most sun, then he could use fewer panels, and the client would save money. Keith installed sunlight sensors on both sides of the roof. Then, he measured the amount of sunlight the sensors on each side of the roof recorded over one sunny summer day.\nFigure: installing solar panels on a roof.</p></div>\n          \n        </div>\n        \n      <div class=\"q-stem\"><p>Which of the following could Keith&#x27;s test show?</p></div>\n      <div class=\"q-diagram-box\"><img class=\"q-figure-img\" src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Official Exam Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n      <div class=\"opts-grid\"><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">A</span><div class=\"opt-text\">the amount of sunlight the roof would get throughout the year</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">B</span><div class=\"opt-text\">how many solar panels could fit on each side of the roof</div></div><div class=\"opt-item correct-opt\"><span class=\"opt-letter\">C</span><div class=\"opt-text\">which side of the roof got more sun over one day</div><span class=\"corr-tag\">✓ Correct</span></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">D</span><div class=\"opt-text\">None of the above</div></div></div>\n      <details class=\"expl-box\">\n        <summary>View Explanation & Provenance (Answer: Option C)</summary>\n        <div class=\"expl-body\">\n          <p>People can use the engineering-design process to develop solutions to problems. One step in the process is testing if a potential solution meets the requirements of the design. How can you determine what a test can show? You need to figure out what was tested and what was measured.\nImagine an engineer needs to design a bridge for a windy location. She wants to make sure the bridge will not move too much in high wind. So, she builds a smaller prototype, or model, of a bridge. Then, she exposes the prototype to high winds and measures how much the bridge moves.\nFirst, identify what was tested. A test can examine one design, or it may compare multiple prototypes to each other. In the test described above, the engineer tested a prototype of a bridge in high wind.\nThen, identify what the test measured. One of the criteria for the bridge was that it not move too much in high winds. The test measured how much the prototype bridge moved.\nTests can show how well one or more designs meet the criteria. The test described above can show whether the bridge would move too much in high winds.</p>\n          \n          <div class=\"hash-row\">\n            <span><strong>Source/License:</strong> ScienceQA Open Figure Benchmark</span>\n            <span><strong>SHA-256:</strong> <code>f16e0cad43fbb5cd5986...</code></span>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </details>\n    </article>\n    \n    <article class=\"q-card\">\n      <div class=\"q-head\">\n        <div class=\"q-badges\">\n          <span class=\"badge b-id\">#65 · SUBLANGENG_Q496215</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-pyq-db\">DB2_PYQ</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-psg\">📖 Passage PSG_F9DACD1541B7 (Question 1 of 2)</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-loc\">UNIVERSAL: ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-subj\">SUB_LANG_ENGLISH — English Language, Reading Comprehension &amp; Verbal Ability</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-diff hard\">Hard</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-pyq\">PYQ: National Standardized English Reading Comprehension Examination (2018-2023)</span><span class=\"badge b-meta\">Body: Official Standardized Examination Board · ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"q-meta\">\n          <span><strong>Domain:</strong> Aptitude &amp; Comprehension</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Unit:</strong> Multi-Question Exam Reading Comprehension Passages</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Topic:</strong> Senior Competitive Exam Comprehension</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      \n        <div class=\"passage-box\">\n          <div class=\"passage-hdr\">\n            <span>📖 Shared Reading Comprehension Passage (high8404)</span>\n            <span class=\"passage-tag\">Shared ID: PSG_F9DACD1541B7 · Question 1 of 2</span>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"passage-body\"><p>Michael is the kind of guy you love to hate. He is always in a good mood and always has something positive to say.</p><p>One day I went up to Michael and asked him, &quot;I don&#x27;t get it. You can&#x27;t be positive all the time. How do you do it?&quot; Michael replied, &quot;Each morning I wake up and say to myself &#x27;Mike, you have two choices today. You can choose to be in a good mood or you can choose to be in a bad mood.&#x27; I choose to be in a good mood. Each time something bad happens, I can choose to be a victim or choose to learn from it. I choose to learn from it. Every time someone comes to me complaining  I can choose to accept their complaining or I can point out the positive side of life. I choose the positive side of life.&quot;</p><p>I often thought about that when I made a choice about life. Several years later, Michael was involved in a serious accident, falling off 60 feet from a communica-tion tower. After l8 hours of operation, and weeks of intensive care, Michael was re-leased from the hospital with rods placed in his back.I asked him what had gone through his mind as the accident took place.</p><p>&quot;The first thing that went through my mind was the well being of my soon-to-born daughter,&quot; Michael replied. &quot;Then, as I lay on the ground, I remembered I had two choices: I could choose to live or I could choose to die. In the operation room, I told the doctors and nurses, &#x27;I&#x27;m choosing to live. Operate on me as if I am alive, not dead&#x27;.&quot;</p><p>Michael lived, thanks to the skill of his doctors, but also because of his amazing attitude.</p></div>\n          \n        </div>\n        \n      <div class=\"q-stem\"><p>Each time something bad happens, Michael chooses to   _  .</p></div>\n      \n      <div class=\"opts-grid\"><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">A</span><div class=\"opt-text\">be in a good mood</div></div><div class=\"opt-item correct-opt\"><span class=\"opt-letter\">B</span><div class=\"opt-text\">learn from it</div><span class=\"corr-tag\">✓ Correct</span></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">C</span><div class=\"opt-text\">complain about it</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">D</span><div class=\"opt-text\">ignore the negative side of it</div></div></div>\n      <details class=\"expl-box\">\n        <summary>View Explanation & Provenance (Answer: Option B)</summary>\n        <div class=\"expl-body\">\n          <p>The passage states that when something bad happens, Michael can choose to be a victim or learn from it, and he explicitly chooses to learn from it. Thus option B is correct. Option A describes his morning mood choice, not his response to bad events. Option C contradicts his positive approach, and option D is not stated.</p>\n          \n          <div class=\"hash-row\">\n            <span><strong>Source/License:</strong> RACE Official Shared-Passage Exam PYQ (high8404) · AI_AUDITED_VERIFIED (g4f:)</span>\n            <span><strong>SHA-256:</strong> <code>72cf73f8f385a6f2cf7f...</code></span>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </details>\n    </article>\n    \n    <article class=\"q-card\">\n      <div class=\"q-head\">\n        <div class=\"q-badges\">\n          <span class=\"badge b-id\">#66 · SUBLANGENG_Q496216</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-pyq-db\">DB2_PYQ</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-psg\">📖 Passage PSG_F9DACD1541B7 (Question 2 of 2)</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-loc\">UNIVERSAL: ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-subj\">SUB_LANG_ENGLISH — English Language, Reading Comprehension &amp; Verbal Ability</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-diff hard\">Hard</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-pyq\">PYQ: National Standardized English Reading Comprehension Examination (2018-2023)</span><span class=\"badge b-meta\">Body: Official Standardized Examination Board · ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"q-meta\">\n          <span><strong>Domain:</strong> Aptitude &amp; Comprehension</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Unit:</strong> Multi-Question Exam Reading Comprehension Passages</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Topic:</strong> Senior Competitive Exam Comprehension</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      \n        <div class=\"passage-box\">\n          <div class=\"passage-hdr\">\n            <span>📖 Shared Reading Comprehension Passage (high8404)</span>\n            <span class=\"passage-tag\">Shared ID: PSG_F9DACD1541B7 · Question 2 of 2</span>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"passage-body\"><p>Michael is the kind of guy you love to hate. He is always in a good mood and always has something positive to say.</p><p>One day I went up to Michael and asked him, &quot;I don&#x27;t get it. You can&#x27;t be positive all the time. How do you do it?&quot; Michael replied, &quot;Each morning I wake up and say to myself &#x27;Mike, you have two choices today. You can choose to be in a good mood or you can choose to be in a bad mood.&#x27; I choose to be in a good mood. Each time something bad happens, I can choose to be a victim or choose to learn from it. I choose to learn from it. Every time someone comes to me complaining  I can choose to accept their complaining or I can point out the positive side of life. I choose the positive side of life.&quot;</p><p>I often thought about that when I made a choice about life. Several years later, Michael was involved in a serious accident, falling off 60 feet from a communica-tion tower. After l8 hours of operation, and weeks of intensive care, Michael was re-leased from the hospital with rods placed in his back.I asked him what had gone through his mind as the accident took place.</p><p>&quot;The first thing that went through my mind was the well being of my soon-to-born daughter,&quot; Michael replied. &quot;Then, as I lay on the ground, I remembered I had two choices: I could choose to live or I could choose to die. In the operation room, I told the doctors and nurses, &#x27;I&#x27;m choosing to live. Operate on me as if I am alive, not dead&#x27;.&quot;</p><p>Michael lived, thanks to the skill of his doctors, but also because of his amazing attitude.</p></div>\n          \n        </div>\n        \n      <div class=\"q-stem\"><p>From the passage we can infer that   _  .</p></div>\n      \n      <div class=\"opts-grid\"><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">A</span><div class=\"opt-text\">most people manage to lead a positive life at any time</div></div><div class=\"opt-item correct-opt\"><span class=\"opt-letter\">B</span><div class=\"opt-text\">most people can&#x27;t lead a positive life at any time</div><span class=\"corr-tag\">✓ Correct</span></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">C</span><div class=\"opt-text\">only Michael can be so positive</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">D</span><div class=\"opt-text\">the author is always a sad person</div></div></div>\n      <details class=\"expl-box\">\n        <summary>View Explanation & Provenance (Answer: Option B)</summary>\n        <div class=\"expl-body\">\n          <p>The narrator initially assumes one cannot be positive all the time, while Michael explains his deliberate choices. The best inference is that most people do not naturally lead a positive life at any time, unlike Michael. Option A contradicts the narrator's assumption. Option C is too absolute because it claims only Michael can be positive. Option D is unsupported because the author's temporary skepticism does not mean he is always sad.</p>\n          \n          <div class=\"hash-row\">\n            <span><strong>Source/License:</strong> RACE Official Shared-Passage Exam PYQ (high8404) · AI_AUDITED_VERIFIED (g4f:)</span>\n            <span><strong>SHA-256:</strong> <code>adbdf4d2bb4cd375ed01...</code></span>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </details>\n    </article>\n    \n    <article class=\"q-card\">\n      <div class=\"q-head\">\n        <div class=\"q-badges\">\n          <span class=\"badge b-id\">#67 · SUBSCIBIOL_Q493681</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-db\">DB1_SCRAPED_MODIFIED</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-psg\">📖 Passage PSG_F9690C22358C (Question 1 of 1)</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-loc\">UNIVERSAL: ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-subj\">SUB_SCI_BIOLOGY — Life Sciences &amp; Biology (Botany, Zoology, Cell Biology, Genetics &amp; Ecology)</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-diff medium\">Medium</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-pyq\">PYQ: Standard Science Curriculum Figure Assessment (2023)</span><span class=\"badge b-meta\">Body: Open Science Curriculum · ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"q-meta\">\n          <span><strong>Domain:</strong> Science</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Unit:</strong> Genes to traits</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Topic:</strong> biology</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      \n        <div class=\"passage-box\">\n          <div class=\"passage-hdr\">\n            <span>📖 Context Passage — Biology</span>\n            <span class=\"passage-tag\">Shared ID: PSG_F9690C22358C · Question 1 of 1</span>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"passage-body\"><p>Read the description of a trait.\nMark has hazel eyes.</p></div>\n          \n        </div>\n        \n      <div class=\"q-stem\"><p>What information supports the conclusion that Mark inherited this trait?</p></div>\n      \n      <div class=\"opts-grid\"><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">A</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Mark&#x27;s friend also has hazel eyes.</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">B</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Mark wears glasses and so do his sisters.</div></div><div class=\"opt-item correct-opt\"><span class=\"opt-letter\">C</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Mark&#x27;s biological father wears contacts in his hazel eyes.</div><span class=\"corr-tag\">✓ Correct</span></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">D</span><div class=\"opt-text\">None of the above</div></div></div>\n      <details class=\"expl-box\">\n        <summary>View Explanation & Provenance (Answer: Option C)</summary>\n        <div class=\"expl-body\">\n          <p>Organisms, including people, have both inherited and acquired traits. Inherited and acquired traits are gained in different ways.\nInherited traits are passed down from biological parents to their offspring through genes. Genes are pieces of hereditary material that contain the instructions that affect inherited traits. Offspring receive their genes, and therefore gain their inherited traits, from their biological parents. Inherited traits do not need to be learned.\nAcquired traits are gained during a person&#x27;s life. Some acquired traits, such as riding a bicycle, are gained by learning. Other acquired traits, such as scars, are caused by the environment. Parents do not pass acquired traits down to their offspring.</p>\n          \n          <div class=\"hash-row\">\n            <span><strong>Source/License:</strong> ScienceQA Open Figure Benchmark</span>\n            <span><strong>SHA-256:</strong> <code>d3d61d6a3f67f253a63a...</code></span>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </details>\n    </article>\n    \n    <article class=\"q-card\">\n      <div class=\"q-head\">\n        <div class=\"q-badges\">\n          <span class=\"badge b-id\">#68 · SUBMEDENT_Q493022</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-db\">DB1_SCRAPED_MODIFIED</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-psg\">📖 Passage PSG_F95E770C083B (Question 1 of 1)</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-fig\">🖼️ Figure / Diagram</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-loc\">UNIVERSAL: ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-subj\">SUB_MED_ENT — Otorhinolaryngology (ENT)</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-diff medium\">Medium</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-pyq\">PYQ: Standard Science Curriculum Figure Assessment (2023)</span><span class=\"badge b-meta\">Body: Open Science Curriculum · ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"q-meta\">\n          <span><strong>Domain:</strong> Medical &amp; Health Sciences</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Unit:</strong> Classification and scientific names</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Topic:</strong> biology</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      \n        <div class=\"passage-box\">\n          <div class=\"passage-hdr\">\n            <span>📖 Context Passage — Biology</span>\n            <span class=\"passage-tag\">Shared ID: PSG_F95E770C083B · Question 1 of 1</span>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"passage-body\"><p>This organism is a plains leopard frog. Its scientific name is Lithobates blairi.</p></div>\n          \n        </div>\n        \n      <div class=\"q-stem\"><p>Select the organism in the same genus as the plains leopard frog.</p></div>\n      <div class=\"q-diagram-box\"><img class=\"q-figure-img\" src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Official Exam Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n      <div class=\"opts-grid\"><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">A</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Hyla cinerea</div></div><div class=\"opt-item correct-opt\"><span class=\"opt-letter\">B</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Lithobates palustris</div><span class=\"corr-tag\">✓ Correct</span></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">C</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Agalychnis callidryas</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">D</span><div class=\"opt-text\">None of the above</div></div></div>\n      <details class=\"expl-box\">\n        <summary>View Explanation & Provenance (Answer: Option B)</summary>\n        <div class=\"expl-body\">\n          <p>Scientists use scientific names to identify organisms. Scientific names are made of two words.\nThe first word in an organism&#x27;s scientific name tells you the organism&#x27;s genus. A genus is a group of organisms that share many traits.\nA genus is made up of one or more species. A species is a group of very similar organisms. The second word in an organism&#x27;s scientific name tells you its species within its genus.\nTogether, the two parts of an organism&#x27;s scientific name identify its species. For example Ursus maritimus and Ursus americanus are two species of bears. They are part of the same genus, Ursus. But they are different species within the genus. Ursus maritimus has the species name maritimus. Ursus americanus has the species name americanus.\nBoth bears have small round ears and sharp claws. But Ursus maritimus has white fur and Ursus americanus has black fur. A plains leopard frog&#x27;s scientific name is Lithobates blairi. The first word of its scientific name is Lithobates.\nLithobates palustris is in the genus Lithobates. The first word of its scientific name is Lithobates. So, Lithobates palustris and Lithobates blairi are in the same genus.\nAgalychnis callidryas is in the genus Agalychnis. The first word of its scientific name is Agalychnis. So, Agalychnis callidryas and Lithobates blairi are not in the same genus.\nHyla cinerea is in the genus Hyla. The first word of its scientific name is Hyla. So, Hyla cinerea and Lithobates blairi are not in the same genus.</p>\n          \n          <div class=\"hash-row\">\n            <span><strong>Source/License:</strong> ScienceQA Open Figure Benchmark</span>\n            <span><strong>SHA-256:</strong> <code>001598ca9ed82e23eb7b...</code></span>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </details>\n    </article>\n    \n    <article class=\"q-card\">\n      <div class=\"q-head\">\n        <div class=\"q-badges\">\n          <span class=\"badge b-id\">#69 · SUBLANGENG_Q495863</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-pyq-db\">DB2_PYQ</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-psg\">📖 Passage PSG_F90F5CF78E12 (Question 1 of 1)</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-loc\">UNIVERSAL: ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-subj\">SUB_LANG_ENGLISH — English Language, Reading Comprehension &amp; Verbal Ability</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-diff hard\">Hard</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-pyq\">PYQ: National Standardized English Reading Comprehension Examination (2018-2023)</span><span class=\"badge b-meta\">Body: Official Standardized Examination Board · ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"q-meta\">\n          <span><strong>Domain:</strong> Aptitude &amp; Comprehension</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Unit:</strong> Multi-Question Exam Reading Comprehension Passages</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Topic:</strong> Senior Competitive Exam Comprehension</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      \n        <div class=\"passage-box\">\n          <div class=\"passage-hdr\">\n            <span>📖 Shared Reading Comprehension Passage (high3337)</span>\n            <span class=\"passage-tag\">Shared ID: PSG_F90F5CF78E12 · Question 1 of 1</span>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"passage-body\"><p>A 16-year-old South Dakota boy who became lost while hunting and spent 16 hours alone in the Black Hills National Forest says he was scared but still managed to make a survival plan.</p><p>Austin DuVall, of Rapid City, became lost on Nov.3 while hunting with his father. He ran after a deer, and soon found himself alone. &quot;I ran after a deer, but I didn&#x27;t get it,&quot; he said. &quot;Then I was really lost.&quot;</p><p>He had only his hunting rifle  and the clothes he was wearing. He had no food or water and had nothing that could help him find his way to safety. &quot;I knew that no one could hear me. I decided to just sleep and get up in the morning and find safety,&quot; he said.</p><p>Austin climbed up on a rock and slept through the night. Then he awoke and relied on skills he learned in a hunter safety course. He followed a stream to an occupied cabin. The couple there called his parents and cooked him a breakfast. &quot; It&#x27;s probably one meal I&#x27;ll never forget for the rest of my life,&quot; he said.</p><p>After DuVall&#x27;s disappearance, a lot of emergency officials and more than 100 volunteers went searching for him. &quot;He wasn&#x27;t sitting there waiting for someone to come and find him,&quot; said his father, Steve DuVall. &quot;We didn&#x27;t find him; he found himself.&quot;</p><p>Mike Kintigh, regional supervisor for the Game, Fish &amp; Parks Department, said one or two hunters will go missing each year, but rarely for more than 24 hours. &quot;We&#x27;re a little bit unique in the Black Hills as it&#x27;s hard to get lost for a very long time. That&#x27;s because we&#x27;ve got so many roads here compared to the Rocky Mountains,&quot; Kintigh said. &quot;You can certainly spend a very uncomfortable night in the woods like Austin did.&quot;</p></div>\n          \n        </div>\n        \n      <div class=\"q-stem\"><p>After Austin realized he lost his way, he   _  .</p></div>\n      \n      <div class=\"opts-grid\"><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">A</span><div class=\"opt-text\">was very nervous but excited</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">B</span><div class=\"opt-text\">cried aloud for help</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">C</span><div class=\"opt-text\">tried to find a safe place</div></div><div class=\"opt-item correct-opt\"><span class=\"opt-letter\">D</span><div class=\"opt-text\">decided to sleep in the wild</div><span class=\"corr-tag\">✓ Correct</span></div></div>\n      <details class=\"expl-box\">\n        <summary>View Explanation & Provenance (Answer: Option D)</summary>\n        <div class=\"expl-body\">\n          <p>The passage follows a logical course of action under duress. After realizing no one could hear him and that he was lost, Austin states his decision to just sleep and get up in the morning to find safety. This direct piece of evidence points to Option D. Option A is contradicted by his fear, B has no support in the text, and C is incorrect as he does not actively search at that moment, but instead resolves to rest before searching the next day.</p>\n          \n          <div class=\"hash-row\">\n            <span><strong>Source/License:</strong> RACE Official Shared-Passage Exam PYQ (high3337) · AI_AUDITED_VERIFIED (g4f:)</span>\n            <span><strong>SHA-256:</strong> <code>48b5ee5c93764a78aabd...</code></span>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </details>\n    </article>\n    \n    <article class=\"q-card\">\n      <div class=\"q-head\">\n        <div class=\"q-badges\">\n          <span class=\"badge b-id\">#70 · SUBMEDENT_Q490939</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-db\">DB1_SCRAPED_MODIFIED</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-psg\">📖 Passage PSG_F8FD6AC55C7D (Question 1 of 1)</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-loc\">UNIVERSAL: ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-subj\">SUB_MED_ENT — Otorhinolaryngology (ENT)</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-diff medium\">Medium</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-pyq\">PYQ: Standard Science Curriculum Figure Assessment (2023)</span><span class=\"badge b-meta\">Body: Open Science Curriculum · ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"q-meta\">\n          <span><strong>Domain:</strong> Medical &amp; Health Sciences</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Unit:</strong> Kinetic and potential energy</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Topic:</strong> physics</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      \n        <div class=\"passage-box\">\n          <div class=\"passage-hdr\">\n            <span>📖 Context Passage — Physics</span>\n            <span class=\"passage-tag\">Shared ID: PSG_F8FD6AC55C7D · Question 1 of 1</span>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"passage-body\"><p>Read the text about a person in motion.\nMatthew rode an escalator from the first floor to the second floor of a shopping mall.</p></div>\n          \n        </div>\n        \n      <div class=\"q-stem\"><p>Complete the statement. Assume that Matthew&#x27;s mass did not change.\nThe gravitational potential energy stored between Matthew and Earth () as he rode the escalator.</p></div>\n      \n      <div class=\"opts-grid\"><div class=\"opt-item correct-opt\"><span class=\"opt-letter\">A</span><div class=\"opt-text\">increased</div><span class=\"corr-tag\">✓ Correct</span></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">B</span><div class=\"opt-text\">stayed the same</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">C</span><div class=\"opt-text\">decreased</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">D</span><div class=\"opt-text\">None of the above</div></div></div>\n      <details class=\"expl-box\">\n        <summary>View Explanation & Provenance (Answer: Option A)</summary>\n        <div class=\"expl-body\">\n          <p>Gravitational potential energy is stored between any two objects. So, for every object on or near Earth, there is gravitational potential energy stored between the object and Earth.\nThe amount of gravitational potential energy stored between an object and Earth depends on the mass of the object. The amount of gravitational potential energy also depends on the distance between the object and the center of Earth. This distance increases when the object moves higher and decreases when the object moves lower.\nIf the distance between an object and the center of Earth changes, the gravitational potential energy stored between the object and Earth will change. The table below shows how this change can happen.\nWhen an object&#x27;s mass stays the same and its distance from the center of Earth... | Gravitational potential energy stored between the object and Earth...\nincreases | increases\ndecreases | decreases\nstays the same | stays the same Think about how the distance between Matthew and the center of Earth changed.\nThe second floor is higher than the first floor. As he rode the escalator toward the second floor, the distance between Matthew and the center of Earth increased. So, the gravitational potential energy stored between Matthew and Earth increased as he rode the escalator.</p>\n          \n          <div class=\"hash-row\">\n            <span><strong>Source/License:</strong> ScienceQA Open Figure Benchmark</span>\n            <span><strong>SHA-256:</strong> <code>2717f893e5b2f87855b6...</code></span>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </details>\n    </article>\n    \n    <article class=\"q-card\">\n      <div class=\"q-head\">\n        <div class=\"q-badges\">\n          <span class=\"badge b-id\">#71 · SUBMEDENT_Q490831</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-db\">DB1_SCRAPED_MODIFIED</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-psg\">📖 Passage PSG_F8C45518693B (Question 1 of 1)</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-loc\">UNIVERSAL: ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-subj\">SUB_MED_ENT — Otorhinolaryngology (ENT)</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-diff medium\">Medium</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-pyq\">PYQ: Standard Science Curriculum Figure Assessment (2023)</span><span class=\"badge b-meta\">Body: Open Science Curriculum · ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"q-meta\">\n          <span><strong>Domain:</strong> Medical &amp; Health Sciences</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Unit:</strong> Designing experiments</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Topic:</strong> science-and-engineering-practices</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      \n        <div class=\"passage-box\">\n          <div class=\"passage-hdr\">\n            <span>📖 Context Passage — Science And Engineering Practices</span>\n            <span class=\"passage-tag\">Shared ID: PSG_F8C45518693B · Question 1 of 1</span>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"passage-body\"><p>Bryan and his classmates are growing tomato plants in the school garden. He wonders what factors affect how tomato plants grow. So, he decides to design an experiment. He has the following supplies available:\nthree different types of tomato seeds\none bag of potting soil\nfive identical clay pots\nwater</p></div>\n          \n        </div>\n        \n      <div class=\"q-stem\"><p>Using only these supplies, which question can Bryan investigate with an experiment?</p></div>\n      \n      <div class=\"opts-grid\"><div class=\"opt-item correct-opt\"><span class=\"opt-letter\">A</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Which of the three types of tomato seeds sprouts the fastest?</div><span class=\"corr-tag\">✓ Correct</span></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">B</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Does a certain kind of tomato plant grow taller when planted in a clay pot or in a plastic pot?</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">C</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Which type of soil will cause a certain kind of tomato plant to grow the most fruit?</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">D</span><div class=\"opt-text\">None of the above</div></div></div>\n      <details class=\"expl-box\">\n        <summary>View Explanation & Provenance (Answer: Option A)</summary>\n        <div class=\"expl-body\">\n          <p>Experiments can be designed to answer specific questions. When designing an experiment, you must identify the supplies that are necessary to answer your question. In order to do this, you need to figure out what will be tested and what will be measured during the experiment.\nImagine that you are wondering if plants grow to different heights when planted in different types of soil. How might you decide what supplies are necessary to conduct this experiment?\nFirst, you need to identify the part of the experiment that will be tested, which is the independent variable. This is usually the part of the experiment that is different or changed. In this case, you would like to know how plants grow in different types of soil. So, you must have different types of soil available.\nNext, you need to identify the part of the experiment that will be measured or observed, which is the dependent variable. In this experiment, you would like to know if some plants grow taller than others. So, you must be able to compare the plants&#x27; heights. To do this, you can observe which plants are taller by looking at them, or you can measure their exact heights with a meterstick.\nSo, if you have different types of soil and can observe or measure the heights of your plants, then you have the supplies you need to investigate your question with an experiment!</p>\n          \n          <div class=\"hash-row\">\n            <span><strong>Source/License:</strong> ScienceQA Open Figure Benchmark</span>\n            <span><strong>SHA-256:</strong> <code>e6e14161f22e9fb06ba9...</code></span>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </details>\n    </article>\n    \n    <article class=\"q-card\">\n      <div class=\"q-head\">\n        <div class=\"q-badges\">\n          <span class=\"badge b-id\">#72 · SUBMEDENT_Q492231</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-db\">DB1_SCRAPED_MODIFIED</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-psg\">📖 Passage PSG_F8B0E0823FFC (Question 1 of 1)</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-loc\">UNIVERSAL: ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-subj\">SUB_MED_ENT — Otorhinolaryngology (ENT)</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-diff medium\">Medium</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-pyq\">PYQ: Standard Science Curriculum Figure Assessment (2023)</span><span class=\"badge b-meta\">Body: Open Science Curriculum · ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"q-meta\">\n          <span><strong>Domain:</strong> Medical &amp; Health Sciences</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Unit:</strong> Kinetic and potential energy</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Topic:</strong> physics</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      \n        <div class=\"passage-box\">\n          <div class=\"passage-hdr\">\n            <span>📖 Context Passage — Physics</span>\n            <span class=\"passage-tag\">Shared ID: PSG_F8B0E0823FFC · Question 1 of 1</span>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"passage-body\"><p>Read the text about an object in motion.\nOn a snowy day, Elizabeth rode her sled down a big hill.</p></div>\n          \n        </div>\n        \n      <div class=\"q-stem\"><p>Complete the statement. Assume that the sled&#x27;s mass did not change.\nThe gravitational potential energy stored between the sled and Earth () as Elizabeth rode down the hill.</p></div>\n      \n      <div class=\"opts-grid\"><div class=\"opt-item correct-opt\"><span class=\"opt-letter\">A</span><div class=\"opt-text\">decreased</div><span class=\"corr-tag\">✓ Correct</span></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">B</span><div class=\"opt-text\">stayed the same</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">C</span><div class=\"opt-text\">increased</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">D</span><div class=\"opt-text\">None of the above</div></div></div>\n      <details class=\"expl-box\">\n        <summary>View Explanation & Provenance (Answer: Option A)</summary>\n        <div class=\"expl-body\">\n          <p>Gravitational potential energy is stored between any two objects. So, for every object on or near Earth, there is gravitational potential energy stored between the object and Earth.\nThe amount of gravitational potential energy stored between an object and Earth depends on the mass of the object. The amount of gravitational potential energy also depends on the distance between the object and the center of Earth. This distance increases when the object moves higher and decreases when the object moves lower.\nIf the distance between an object and the center of Earth changes, the gravitational potential energy stored between the object and Earth will change. The table below shows how this change can happen.\nWhen an object&#x27;s mass stays the same and its distance from the center of Earth... | Gravitational potential energy stored between the object and Earth...\nincreases | increases\ndecreases | decreases\nstays the same | stays the same Think about how the distance between the sled and the center of Earth changed.\nThe bottom of the hill was lower than the point where Elizabeth started sledding. As Elizabeth rode toward the bottom of the hill, the distance between the sled and the center of Earth decreased. So, the gravitational potential energy stored between the sled and Earth decreased as Elizabeth rode down the hill.</p>\n          \n          <div class=\"hash-row\">\n            <span><strong>Source/License:</strong> ScienceQA Open Figure Benchmark</span>\n            <span><strong>SHA-256:</strong> <code>eba838fde6204a2e7064...</code></span>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </details>\n    </article>\n    \n    <article class=\"q-card\">\n      <div class=\"q-head\">\n        <div class=\"q-badges\">\n          <span class=\"badge b-id\">#73 · SUBMEENGME_Q492251</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-db\">DB1_SCRAPED_MODIFIED</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-psg\">📖 Passage PSG_F8A48D1AB00C (Question 1 of 1)</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-fig\">🖼️ Figure / Diagram</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-loc\">UNIVERSAL: ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-subj\">SUB_ME_ENG_MECH — Engineering Mechanics &amp; Strength of Materials</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-diff medium\">Medium</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-pyq\">PYQ: Standard Science Curriculum Figure Assessment (2023)</span><span class=\"badge b-meta\">Body: Open Science Curriculum · ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"q-meta\">\n          <span><strong>Domain:</strong> Engineering</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Unit:</strong> Engineering practices</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Topic:</strong> science-and-engineering-practices</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      \n        <div class=\"passage-box\">\n          <div class=\"passage-hdr\">\n            <span>📖 Context Passage — Science And Engineering Practices</span>\n            <span class=\"passage-tag\">Shared ID: PSG_F8A48D1AB00C · Question 1 of 1</span>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"passage-body\"><p>People can use the engineering-design process to develop solutions to problems. One step in the process is testing if a potential solution meets the requirements of the design.\nThe passage below describes how the engineering-design process was used to test a solution to a problem. Read the passage. Then answer the question below.\n\nMarvin, a materials engineer, was developing a quick-drying fabric for athletic clothing. The fabric needed to absorb one drop of water in less than one second and dry completely in less than five minutes. Marvin thought a fabric made from cotton and polyester would both absorb water well and dry quickly. But he needed to decide what percentage of each material to use.\nSo, he made a sample fabric that was 50% cotton and 50% polyester. Then he put one drop of water on the sample. He timed how long it took the fabric to dry after the water was absorbed.\nFigure: fabric that has not absorbed drops of water.</p></div>\n          \n        </div>\n        \n      <div class=\"q-stem\"><p>Which of the following could Marvin&#x27;s test show?</p></div>\n      <div class=\"q-diagram-box\"><img class=\"q-figure-img\" src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Official Exam Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n      <div class=\"opts-grid\"><div class=\"opt-item correct-opt\"><span class=\"opt-letter\">A</span><div class=\"opt-text\">how long it would take the sample fabric to dry after it absorbed one drop of water</div><span class=\"corr-tag\">✓ Correct</span></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">B</span><div class=\"opt-text\">if the sample fabric would absorb one drop of water in less than one second</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">C</span><div class=\"opt-text\">how much athletes would sweat in the fabric</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">D</span><div class=\"opt-text\">None of the above</div></div></div>\n      <details class=\"expl-box\">\n        <summary>View Explanation & Provenance (Answer: Option A)</summary>\n        <div class=\"expl-body\">\n          <p>People can use the engineering-design process to develop solutions to problems. One step in the process is testing if a potential solution meets the requirements of the design. How can you determine what a test can show? You need to figure out what was tested and what was measured.\nImagine an engineer needs to design a bridge for a windy location. She wants to make sure the bridge will not move too much in high wind. So, she builds a smaller prototype, or model, of a bridge. Then, she exposes the prototype to high winds and measures how much the bridge moves.\nFirst, identify what was tested. A test can examine one design, or it may compare multiple prototypes to each other. In the test described above, the engineer tested a prototype of a bridge in high wind.\nThen, identify what the test measured. One of the criteria for the bridge was that it not move too much in high winds. The test measured how much the prototype bridge moved.\nTests can show how well one or more designs meet the criteria. The test described above can show whether the bridge would move too much in high winds.</p>\n          \n          <div class=\"hash-row\">\n            <span><strong>Source/License:</strong> ScienceQA Open Figure Benchmark</span>\n            <span><strong>SHA-256:</strong> <code>c21f2f742ea6459f6539...</code></span>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </details>\n    </article>\n    \n    <article class=\"q-card\">\n      <div class=\"q-head\">\n        <div class=\"q-badges\">\n          <span class=\"badge b-id\">#74 · SUBMEDENT_Q489712</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-db\">DB1_SCRAPED_MODIFIED</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-psg\">📖 Passage PSG_F88D10BD4DE0 (Question 1 of 1)</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-loc\">UNIVERSAL: ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-subj\">SUB_MED_ENT — Otorhinolaryngology (ENT)</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-diff medium\">Medium</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-pyq\">PYQ: Standard Science Curriculum Figure Assessment (2023)</span><span class=\"badge b-meta\">Body: Open Science Curriculum · ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"q-meta\">\n          <span><strong>Domain:</strong> Medical &amp; Health Sciences</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Unit:</strong> Designing experiments</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Topic:</strong> science-and-engineering-practices</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      \n        <div class=\"passage-box\">\n          <div class=\"passage-hdr\">\n            <span>📖 Context Passage — Science And Engineering Practices</span>\n            <span class=\"passage-tag\">Shared ID: PSG_F88D10BD4DE0 · Question 1 of 1</span>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"passage-body\"><p>Mia leaves a glass jar of cold tea outside in full sunlight. When she goes to get it, she notices that the tea is warm. She wonders what factors affect how warm a liquid gets from sitting in the sunlight. So, she decides to design an experiment. She has the following supplies available:\ntwo identical glass jars\ntwo jar lids\ntap water\na measuring cup\ntwo thermometers</p></div>\n          \n        </div>\n        \n      <div class=\"q-stem\"><p>Using only these supplies, which question can Mia investigate with an experiment?</p></div>\n      \n      <div class=\"opts-grid\"><div class=\"opt-item correct-opt\"><span class=\"opt-letter\">A</span><div class=\"opt-text\">When placed in the sun, will eight ounces of water in a closed jar or eight ounces of water in an open jar get warmer?</div><span class=\"corr-tag\">✓ Correct</span></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">B</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Will eight ounces of carbonated water or eight ounces of tap water get warmer when placed in a jar in the sun?</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">C</span><div class=\"opt-text\">When placed in the sun, will eight ounces of water in a glass jar or eight ounces of water in a plastic cup get warmer?</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">D</span><div class=\"opt-text\">None of the above</div></div></div>\n      <details class=\"expl-box\">\n        <summary>View Explanation & Provenance (Answer: Option A)</summary>\n        <div class=\"expl-body\">\n          <p>Experiments can be designed to answer specific questions. When designing an experiment, you must identify the supplies that are necessary to answer your question. In order to do this, you need to figure out what will be tested and what will be measured during the experiment.\nImagine that you are wondering if plants grow to different heights when planted in different types of soil. How might you decide what supplies are necessary to conduct this experiment?\nFirst, you need to identify the part of the experiment that will be tested, which is the independent variable. This is usually the part of the experiment that is different or changed. In this case, you would like to know how plants grow in different types of soil. So, you must have different types of soil available.\nNext, you need to identify the part of the experiment that will be measured or observed, which is the dependent variable. In this experiment, you would like to know if some plants grow taller than others. So, you must be able to compare the plants&#x27; heights. To do this, you can observe which plants are taller by looking at them, or you can measure their exact heights with a meterstick.\nSo, if you have different types of soil and can observe or measure the heights of your plants, then you have the supplies you need to investigate your question with an experiment!</p>\n          \n          <div class=\"hash-row\">\n            <span><strong>Source/License:</strong> ScienceQA Open Figure Benchmark</span>\n            <span><strong>SHA-256:</strong> <code>bc1c8be0cb7fe0bef340...</code></span>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </details>\n    </article>\n    \n    <article class=\"q-card\">\n      <div class=\"q-head\">\n        <div class=\"q-badges\">\n          <span class=\"badge b-id\">#75 · SUBMEENGME_Q490641</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-db\">DB1_SCRAPED_MODIFIED</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-psg\">📖 Passage PSG_F85CE1759F2A (Question 1 of 1)</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-fig\">🖼️ Figure / Diagram</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-loc\">UNIVERSAL: ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-subj\">SUB_ME_ENG_MECH — Engineering Mechanics &amp; Strength of Materials</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-diff medium\">Medium</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-pyq\">PYQ: Standard Science Curriculum Figure Assessment (2023)</span><span class=\"badge b-meta\">Body: Open Science Curriculum · ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"q-meta\">\n          <span><strong>Domain:</strong> Engineering</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Unit:</strong> Engineering practices</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Topic:</strong> science-and-engineering-practices</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      \n        <div class=\"passage-box\">\n          <div class=\"passage-hdr\">\n            <span>📖 Context Passage — Science And Engineering Practices</span>\n            <span class=\"passage-tag\">Shared ID: PSG_F85CE1759F2A · Question 1 of 1</span>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"passage-body\"><p>People can use the engineering-design process to develop solutions to problems. One step in the process is testing if a potential solution meets the requirements of the design.\nThe passage below describes how the engineering-design process was used to test a solution to a problem. Read the passage. Then answer the question below.\n\nWind turbines use wind power to produce electricity. Akira was a materials engineer who designed wind turbines. She wanted to design a new turbine that would produce 10% more electricity than older wind turbines. She thought that a turbine made from lightweight material would turn more easily and produce more electricity. So, Akira created a computer model of a turbine made from lightweight material. Then she used the model to calculate how much more electricity the new turbine could produce compared to the older turbines.\nFigure: studying a wind turbine computer model.</p></div>\n          \n        </div>\n        \n      <div class=\"q-stem\"><p>Which of the following could Akira&#x27;s test show?</p></div>\n      <div class=\"q-diagram-box\"><img class=\"q-figure-img\" src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Official Exam Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n      <div class=\"opts-grid\"><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">A</span><div class=\"opt-text\">how much the new turbine would weigh</div></div><div class=\"opt-item correct-opt\"><span class=\"opt-letter\">B</span><div class=\"opt-text\">whether the new turbine could produce 10% more electricity</div><span class=\"corr-tag\">✓ Correct</span></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">C</span><div class=\"opt-text\">if the new turbine could turn easily</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">D</span><div class=\"opt-text\">None of the above</div></div></div>\n      <details class=\"expl-box\">\n        <summary>View Explanation & Provenance (Answer: Option B)</summary>\n        <div class=\"expl-body\">\n          <p>People can use the engineering-design process to develop solutions to problems. One step in the process is testing if a potential solution meets the requirements of the design. How can you determine what a test can show? You need to figure out what was tested and what was measured.\nImagine an engineer needs to design a bridge for a windy location. She wants to make sure the bridge will not move too much in high wind. So, she builds a smaller prototype, or model, of a bridge. Then, she exposes the prototype to high winds and measures how much the bridge moves.\nFirst, identify what was tested. A test can examine one design, or it may compare multiple prototypes to each other. In the test described above, the engineer tested a prototype of a bridge in high wind.\nThen, identify what the test measured. One of the criteria for the bridge was that it not move too much in high winds. The test measured how much the prototype bridge moved.\nTests can show how well one or more designs meet the criteria. The test described above can show whether the bridge would move too much in high winds.</p>\n          \n          <div class=\"hash-row\">\n            <span><strong>Source/License:</strong> ScienceQA Open Figure Benchmark</span>\n            <span><strong>SHA-256:</strong> <code>0a44f92c712044d90a2b...</code></span>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </details>\n    </article>\n    \n    <article class=\"q-card\">\n      <div class=\"q-head\">\n        <div class=\"q-badges\">\n          <span class=\"badge b-id\">#76 · SUBLANGENG_Q496377</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-pyq-db\">DB2_PYQ</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-psg\">📖 Passage PSG_F83AA8B5176D (Question 1 of 2)</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-loc\">UNIVERSAL: ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-subj\">SUB_LANG_ENGLISH — English Language, Reading Comprehension &amp; Verbal Ability</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-diff hard\">Hard</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-pyq\">PYQ: National Standardized English Reading Comprehension Examination (2018-2023)</span><span class=\"badge b-meta\">Body: Official Standardized Examination Board · ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"q-meta\">\n          <span><strong>Domain:</strong> Aptitude &amp; Comprehension</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Unit:</strong> Multi-Question Exam Reading Comprehension Passages</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Topic:</strong> Senior Competitive Exam Comprehension</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      \n        <div class=\"passage-box\">\n          <div class=\"passage-hdr\">\n            <span>📖 Shared Reading Comprehension Passage (high23064)</span>\n            <span class=\"passage-tag\">Shared ID: PSG_F83AA8B5176D · Question 1 of 2</span>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"passage-body\"><p>I was dirty smelly hungry and some somewhere beneath all that, suntanned .It was the end of an Inter-Rail holiday.My body couldn&#x27;t take any more punishment. My mind couldn&#x27;t deal with any more foreign timetables, currencies or languages.</p><p>&quot; _ ,&quot; I said, as I stepped onto home ground.I said exactly the same thing the following year.And the next, all I had to do was buy one train ticket and, because I was under twenty--five years old,I could spend a whole month going anywhere I wanted in Europe. Ordinary beds are never the same once you&#x27;ve learnt to sleep, in the passage of a train, the rhythm rocking you into a deep sleep.</p><p>Carrying all your possessions on your back  in a tucksack  makes you have a vefy basic approach to travel,and encourages incredible wastefulness that can lead  to burning socks that have become too anti-social ,and getting rid of books when finished.On the other hand, _ is entirely in the spirit of Inter--Rail,for common sense and reasoning can be thrown out of the window along with the paperback book and the socks.All it takes to achieve this carefree attitude is one of those tickets in your hand.</p><p>Any system that enables young people to travef through countries at a rate of more than one a day must be pretty social on that first trip,my friehds mad I were at first unaware of the possibilities of this type of train ticket,thinking it was just an inexpensive way of getting to and from our chosen camp-site in southern France.But the idea of non-stop travel proved too attractive,for there was always just one more country over the border,always that little bit further to go.And what did the extra miles cost us? Nothing.</p><p>We were not completely uninterested in culture.But this was a first holiday without parents,as it was for most other Inter-Railers,and in organizing our own timetable  we left out everything except the most available sights.This was the chance to escape the guided tour,an opportunity to do something different.I took great pride in the fact that.in many places,all I could be bothered to see was the view from the station.We were just there to get by,and to have good time doing so.In this we were not different from most of the other Inter-Raiders with whom we shared passage  floors,food and water,money and music.</p><p>The excitement of travel comes from the sudden reality of somewhere that was previously just a name.It is as if the city in which you arrive never actually existed until the train pulls in at the station and you are able to see it with your own tired eyes for the first time.</p></div>\n          \n        </div>\n        \n      <div class=\"q-stem\"><p>At the end of his first trip, the writer said&quot;Never again&quot;because</p></div>\n      \n      <div class=\"opts-grid\"><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">A</span><div class=\"opt-text\">He fell ill</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">B</span><div class=\"opt-text\">he disliked trains</div></div><div class=\"opt-item correct-opt\"><span class=\"opt-letter\">C</span><div class=\"opt-text\">he was tired from the journey</div><span class=\"corr-tag\">✓ Correct</span></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">D</span><div class=\"opt-text\">he had lost money</div></div></div>\n      <details class=\"expl-box\">\n        <summary>View Explanation & Provenance (Answer: Option C)</summary>\n        <div class=\"expl-body\">\n          <p>The passage states the writer’s body could not take more punishment and his mind could not handle foreign timetables, currencies, or languages. This physical and mental exhaustion explains the ‘Never again’ remark. Option C captures that fatigue. Option A is unsupported because illness is not mentioned; B is wrong because trains are later enjoyed; D is unsupported because no money is lost.</p>\n          \n          <div class=\"hash-row\">\n            <span><strong>Source/License:</strong> RACE Official Shared-Passage Exam PYQ (high23064) · AI_AUDITED_VERIFIED (g4f:)</span>\n            <span><strong>SHA-256:</strong> <code>9065087e71fd7c3b70aa...</code></span>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </details>\n    </article>\n    \n    <article class=\"q-card\">\n      <div class=\"q-head\">\n        <div class=\"q-badges\">\n          <span class=\"badge b-id\">#77 · SUBLANGENG_Q496378</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-pyq-db\">DB2_PYQ</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-psg\">📖 Passage PSG_F83AA8B5176D (Question 2 of 2)</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-loc\">UNIVERSAL: ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-subj\">SUB_LANG_ENGLISH — English Language, Reading Comprehension &amp; Verbal Ability</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-diff hard\">Hard</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-pyq\">PYQ: National Standardized English Reading Comprehension Examination (2018-2023)</span><span class=\"badge b-meta\">Body: Official Standardized Examination Board · ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"q-meta\">\n          <span><strong>Domain:</strong> Aptitude &amp; Comprehension</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Unit:</strong> Multi-Question Exam Reading Comprehension Passages</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Topic:</strong> Senior Competitive Exam Comprehension</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      \n        <div class=\"passage-box\">\n          <div class=\"passage-hdr\">\n            <span>📖 Shared Reading Comprehension Passage (high23064)</span>\n            <span class=\"passage-tag\">Shared ID: PSG_F83AA8B5176D · Question 2 of 2</span>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"passage-body\"><p>I was dirty smelly hungry and some somewhere beneath all that, suntanned .It was the end of an Inter-Rail holiday.My body couldn&#x27;t take any more punishment. My mind couldn&#x27;t deal with any more foreign timetables, currencies or languages.</p><p>&quot; _ ,&quot; I said, as I stepped onto home ground.I said exactly the same thing the following year.And the next, all I had to do was buy one train ticket and, because I was under twenty--five years old,I could spend a whole month going anywhere I wanted in Europe. Ordinary beds are never the same once you&#x27;ve learnt to sleep, in the passage of a train, the rhythm rocking you into a deep sleep.</p><p>Carrying all your possessions on your back  in a tucksack  makes you have a vefy basic approach to travel,and encourages incredible wastefulness that can lead  to burning socks that have become too anti-social ,and getting rid of books when finished.On the other hand, _ is entirely in the spirit of Inter--Rail,for common sense and reasoning can be thrown out of the window along with the paperback book and the socks.All it takes to achieve this carefree attitude is one of those tickets in your hand.</p><p>Any system that enables young people to travef through countries at a rate of more than one a day must be pretty social on that first trip,my friehds mad I were at first unaware of the possibilities of this type of train ticket,thinking it was just an inexpensive way of getting to and from our chosen camp-site in southern France.But the idea of non-stop travel proved too attractive,for there was always just one more country over the border,always that little bit further to go.And what did the extra miles cost us? Nothing.</p><p>We were not completely uninterested in culture.But this was a first holiday without parents,as it was for most other Inter-Railers,and in organizing our own timetable  we left out everything except the most available sights.This was the chance to escape the guided tour,an opportunity to do something different.I took great pride in the fact that.in many places,all I could be bothered to see was the view from the station.We were just there to get by,and to have good time doing so.In this we were not different from most of the other Inter-Raiders with whom we shared passage  floors,food and water,money and music.</p><p>The excitement of travel comes from the sudden reality of somewhere that was previously just a name.It is as if the city in which you arrive never actually existed until the train pulls in at the station and you are able to see it with your own tired eyes for the first time.</p></div>\n          \n        </div>\n        \n      <div class=\"q-stem\"><p>Why did the writer originally buy an Inter--Rail ticket?</p></div>\n      \n      <div class=\"opts-grid\"><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">A</span><div class=\"opt-text\">To get to one place cheaply.</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">B</span><div class=\"opt-text\">To meet other young people.</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">C</span><div class=\"opt-text\">To see a lot of famous places</div></div><div class=\"opt-item correct-opt\"><span class=\"opt-letter\">D</span><div class=\"opt-text\">To go on a tour of  Europe.</div><span class=\"corr-tag\">✓ Correct</span></div></div>\n      <details class=\"expl-box\">\n        <summary>View Explanation & Provenance (Answer: Option D)</summary>\n        <div class=\"expl-body\">\n          <p>The excerpt says one ticket let him spend a whole month going anywhere he wanted in Europe, so the ticket was for touring Europe, not one cheap destination. Thus D is correct. A contradicts the anywhere wording; B and C introduce motives not stated in the passage; the benefit described is broad European travel.</p>\n          \n          <div class=\"hash-row\">\n            <span><strong>Source/License:</strong> RACE Official Shared-Passage Exam PYQ (high23064) · AI_AUDITED_VERIFIED (g4f:)</span>\n            <span><strong>SHA-256:</strong> <code>96dbc0bafbe3b9812324...</code></span>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </details>\n    </article>\n    \n    <article class=\"q-card\">\n      <div class=\"q-head\">\n        <div class=\"q-badges\">\n          <span class=\"badge b-id\">#78 · SUBSCIBIOL_Q490005</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-db\">DB1_SCRAPED_MODIFIED</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-psg\">📖 Passage PSG_F8180CB3550B (Question 1 of 1)</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-loc\">UNIVERSAL: ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-subj\">SUB_SCI_BIOLOGY — Life Sciences &amp; Biology (Botany, Zoology, Cell Biology, Genetics &amp; Ecology)</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-diff medium\">Medium</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-pyq\">PYQ: Standard Science Curriculum Figure Assessment (2023)</span><span class=\"badge b-meta\">Body: Open Science Curriculum · ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"q-meta\">\n          <span><strong>Domain:</strong> Science</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Unit:</strong> Genes to traits</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Topic:</strong> biology</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      \n        <div class=\"passage-box\">\n          <div class=\"passage-hdr\">\n            <span>📖 Context Passage — Biology</span>\n            <span class=\"passage-tag\">Shared ID: PSG_F8180CB3550B · Question 1 of 1</span>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"passage-body\"><p>Read the description of a trait.\nVivian can play the cello.</p></div>\n          \n        </div>\n        \n      <div class=\"q-stem\"><p>What information supports the conclusion that Vivian acquired this trait?</p></div>\n      \n      <div class=\"opts-grid\"><div class=\"opt-item correct-opt\"><span class=\"opt-letter\">A</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Vivian learned how to play the cello in music class.</div><span class=\"corr-tag\">✓ Correct</span></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">B</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Vivian and her father play the cello together.</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">C</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Vivian knows how to polish her cello.</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">D</span><div class=\"opt-text\">None of the above</div></div></div>\n      <details class=\"expl-box\">\n        <summary>View Explanation & Provenance (Answer: Option A)</summary>\n        <div class=\"expl-body\">\n          <p>Organisms, including people, have both inherited and acquired traits. Inherited and acquired traits are gained in different ways.\nInherited traits are passed down from biological parents to their offspring through genes. Genes are pieces of hereditary material that contain the instructions that affect inherited traits. Offspring receive their genes, and therefore gain their inherited traits, from their biological parents. Inherited traits do not need to be learned.\nAcquired traits are gained during a person&#x27;s life. Some acquired traits, such as riding a bicycle, are gained by learning. Other acquired traits, such as scars, are caused by the environment. Parents do not pass acquired traits down to their offspring.</p>\n          \n          <div class=\"hash-row\">\n            <span><strong>Source/License:</strong> ScienceQA Open Figure Benchmark</span>\n            <span><strong>SHA-256:</strong> <code>2b34a1a6dc92dd2e287c...</code></span>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </details>\n    </article>\n    \n    <article class=\"q-card\">\n      <div class=\"q-head\">\n        <div class=\"q-badges\">\n          <span class=\"badge b-id\">#79 · SUBMEENGME_Q491426</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-db\">DB1_SCRAPED_MODIFIED</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-psg\">📖 Passage PSG_F805DDC932B4 (Question 1 of 1)</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-fig\">🖼️ Figure / Diagram</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-loc\">UNIVERSAL: ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-subj\">SUB_ME_ENG_MECH — Engineering Mechanics &amp; Strength of Materials</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-diff medium\">Medium</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-pyq\">PYQ: Standard Science Curriculum Figure Assessment (2023)</span><span class=\"badge b-meta\">Body: Open Science Curriculum · ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"q-meta\">\n          <span><strong>Domain:</strong> Engineering</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Unit:</strong> Engineering practices</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Topic:</strong> science-and-engineering-practices</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      \n        <div class=\"passage-box\">\n          <div class=\"passage-hdr\">\n            <span>📖 Context Passage — Science And Engineering Practices</span>\n            <span class=\"passage-tag\">Shared ID: PSG_F805DDC932B4 · Question 1 of 1</span>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"passage-body\"><p>People can use the engineering-design process to develop solutions to problems. One step in the process is testing if a potential solution meets the requirements of the design.\nThe passage below describes how the engineering-design process was used to test a solution to a problem. Read the passage. Then answer the question below.\n\nPeople with diabetes sometimes take a medicine made from insulin. Insulin can be made by a special type of bacteria. Justine was a bioengineer who wanted to increase the amount of insulin that the bacteria produced by 20%. She read that giving the bacteria more nutrients could affect the amount of insulin they produced. So, Justine gave extra nutrients to some of the bacteria. Then, she measured how much insulin those bacteria produced compared to bacteria that did not get extra nutrients.\nFigure: studying bacteria in a laboratory.</p></div>\n          \n        </div>\n        \n      <div class=\"q-stem\"><p>Which of the following could Justine&#x27;s test show?</p></div>\n      <div class=\"q-diagram-box\"><img class=\"q-figure-img\" src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Official Exam Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n      <div class=\"opts-grid\"><div class=\"opt-item correct-opt\"><span class=\"opt-letter\">A</span><div class=\"opt-text\">whether she added enough nutrients to help the bacteria produce 20% more insulin</div><span class=\"corr-tag\">✓ Correct</span></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">B</span><div class=\"opt-text\">whether producing more insulin would help the bacteria grow faster</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">C</span><div class=\"opt-text\">whether different types of bacteria would need different nutrients to produce insulin</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">D</span><div class=\"opt-text\">None of the above</div></div></div>\n      <details class=\"expl-box\">\n        <summary>View Explanation & Provenance (Answer: Option A)</summary>\n        <div class=\"expl-body\">\n          <p>People can use the engineering-design process to develop solutions to problems. One step in the process is testing if a potential solution meets the requirements of the design. How can you determine what a test can show? You need to figure out what was tested and what was measured.\nImagine an engineer needs to design a bridge for a windy location. She wants to make sure the bridge will not move too much in high wind. So, she builds a smaller prototype, or model, of a bridge. Then, she exposes the prototype to high winds and measures how much the bridge moves.\nFirst, identify what was tested. A test can examine one design, or it may compare multiple prototypes to each other. In the test described above, the engineer tested a prototype of a bridge in high wind.\nThen, identify what the test measured. One of the criteria for the bridge was that it not move too much in high winds. The test measured how much the prototype bridge moved.\nTests can show how well one or more designs meet the criteria. The test described above can show whether the bridge would move too much in high winds.</p>\n          \n          <div class=\"hash-row\">\n            <span><strong>Source/License:</strong> ScienceQA Open Figure Benchmark</span>\n            <span><strong>SHA-256:</strong> <code>2f04d76c916943dfe88d...</code></span>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </details>\n    </article>\n    \n    <article class=\"q-card\">\n      <div class=\"q-head\">\n        <div class=\"q-badges\">\n          <span class=\"badge b-id\">#80 · SUBMEENGME_Q495257</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-db\">DB1_SCRAPED_MODIFIED</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-psg\">📖 Passage PSG_F7F7CBC30FCA (Question 1 of 1)</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-fig\">🖼️ Figure / Diagram</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-loc\">UNIVERSAL: ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-subj\">SUB_ME_ENG_MECH — Engineering Mechanics &amp; Strength of Materials</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-diff medium\">Medium</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-pyq\">PYQ: Standard Science Curriculum Figure Assessment (2023)</span><span class=\"badge b-meta\">Body: Open Science Curriculum · ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"q-meta\">\n          <span><strong>Domain:</strong> Engineering</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Unit:</strong> Engineering practices</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Topic:</strong> science-and-engineering-practices</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      \n        <div class=\"passage-box\">\n          <div class=\"passage-hdr\">\n            <span>📖 Context Passage — Science And Engineering Practices</span>\n            <span class=\"passage-tag\">Shared ID: PSG_F7F7CBC30FCA · Question 1 of 1</span>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"passage-body\"><p>People can use the engineering-design process to develop solutions to problems. One step in the process is testing if a potential solution meets the requirements of the design.\nThe passage below describes how the engineering-design process was used to test a solution to a problem. Read the passage. Then answer the question below.\n\nEli was installing solar panels on the roof of a client&#x27;s house. The panels had to provide enough electricity to power the house year-round. Eli needed to decide how many panels to install and which side of the roof to install them on. If he put the panels on the side that got the most sun, then he could use fewer panels, and the client would save money. Eli installed sunlight sensors on both sides of the roof. Then, he measured the amount of sunlight the sensors on each side of the roof recorded over one sunny summer day.\nFigure: installing solar panels on a roof.</p></div>\n          \n        </div>\n        \n      <div class=\"q-stem\"><p>Which of the following could Eli&#x27;s test show?</p></div>\n      <div class=\"q-diagram-box\"><img class=\"q-figure-img\" src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Official Exam Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n      <div class=\"opts-grid\"><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">A</span><div class=\"opt-text\">the amount of sunlight the roof would get throughout the year</div></div><div class=\"opt-item correct-opt\"><span class=\"opt-letter\">B</span><div class=\"opt-text\">which side of the roof got more sun over one day</div><span class=\"corr-tag\">✓ Correct</span></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">C</span><div class=\"opt-text\">how many solar panels could fit on each side of the roof</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">D</span><div class=\"opt-text\">None of the above</div></div></div>\n      <details class=\"expl-box\">\n        <summary>View Explanation & Provenance (Answer: Option B)</summary>\n        <div class=\"expl-body\">\n          <p>People can use the engineering-design process to develop solutions to problems. One step in the process is testing if a potential solution meets the requirements of the design. How can you determine what a test can show? You need to figure out what was tested and what was measured.\nImagine an engineer needs to design a bridge for a windy location. She wants to make sure the bridge will not move too much in high wind. So, she builds a smaller prototype, or model, of a bridge. Then, she exposes the prototype to high winds and measures how much the bridge moves.\nFirst, identify what was tested. A test can examine one design, or it may compare multiple prototypes to each other. In the test described above, the engineer tested a prototype of a bridge in high wind.\nThen, identify what the test measured. One of the criteria for the bridge was that it not move too much in high winds. The test measured how much the prototype bridge moved.\nTests can show how well one or more designs meet the criteria. The test described above can show whether the bridge would move too much in high winds.</p>\n          \n          <div class=\"hash-row\">\n            <span><strong>Source/License:</strong> ScienceQA Open Figure Benchmark</span>\n            <span><strong>SHA-256:</strong> <code>5cd8b16dce9dd861835d...</code></span>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </details>\n    </article>\n    \n    <article class=\"q-card\">\n      <div class=\"q-head\">\n        <div class=\"q-badges\">\n          <span class=\"badge b-id\">#81 · SUBSCIPHYS_Q495228</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-db\">DB1_SCRAPED_MODIFIED</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-psg\">📖 Passage PSG_F79813669B05 (Question 1 of 1)</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-fig\">🖼️ Figure / Diagram</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-loc\">UNIVERSAL: ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-subj\">SUB_SCI_PHYSICS — General Science (Physics, Chemistry, Biology &amp; Geography)</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-diff medium\">Medium</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-pyq\">PYQ: Standard Science Curriculum Figure Assessment (2023)</span><span class=\"badge b-meta\">Body: Open Science Curriculum · ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"q-meta\">\n          <span><strong>Domain:</strong> Science</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Unit:</strong> Fossils</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Topic:</strong> earth-science</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      \n        <div class=\"passage-box\">\n          <div class=\"passage-hdr\">\n            <span>📖 Context Passage — Earth Science</span>\n            <span class=\"passage-tag\">Shared ID: PSG_F79813669B05 · Question 1 of 1</span>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"passage-body\"><p>This picture shows a fossil of an ancient animal called Miocidaris.\nMiocidaris lived in the ocean over 230,000,000 years ago.</p></div>\n          \n        </div>\n        \n      <div class=\"q-stem\"><p>Which trait did Miocidaris have? Select the trait you can observe on the fossil.</p></div>\n      <div class=\"q-diagram-box\"><img class=\"q-figure-img\" src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Official Exam Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n      <div class=\"opts-grid\"><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">A</span><div class=\"opt-text\">a mostly orange body</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">B</span><div class=\"opt-text\">a mushroom-shaped body</div></div><div class=\"opt-item correct-opt\"><span class=\"opt-letter\">C</span><div class=\"opt-text\">straight spines</div><span class=\"corr-tag\">✓ Correct</span></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">D</span><div class=\"opt-text\">None of the above</div></div></div>\n      <details class=\"expl-box\">\n        <summary>View Explanation & Provenance (Answer: Option C)</summary>\n        <div class=\"expl-body\">\n          <p>The way an organism looks or acts is called a trait. Scientists use fossils to learn more about the traits of ancient organisms.\nFossils can preserve the remains of body parts and activities. A fossil of a body part, such as a tail or a wing, can tell you what an organism looked like. A fossil of an organism&#x27;s activities, such as a burrow or a footprint, can tell you about the organism&#x27;s behavior.\nHere are three examples of fossils and the traits that you can observe from them:\nThis is a fossil of an animal. This fossil tells you that the animal had a spiral-shaped shell.\nThis is a fossil of a plant. This fossil tells you that the plant had small leaves arranged in a branched pattern.\nThis is a fossil of an animal&#x27;s footprint. This fossil tells you that the animal could walk on land.\nAn organism&#x27;s fossil may not show all of the organism&#x27;s traits. This is because most body parts are destroyed during fossil formation. When an organism&#x27;s body turns into a fossil, only a few body parts are usually preserved.</p>\n          \n          <div class=\"hash-row\">\n            <span><strong>Source/License:</strong> ScienceQA Open Figure Benchmark</span>\n            <span><strong>SHA-256:</strong> <code>7a2ad7f14d545a627cf9...</code></span>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </details>\n    </article>\n    \n    <article class=\"q-card\">\n      <div class=\"q-head\">\n        <div class=\"q-badges\">\n          <span class=\"badge b-id\">#82 · SUBMEDPSM_Q491150</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-db\">DB1_SCRAPED_MODIFIED</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-psg\">📖 Passage PSG_F78B53FD94DB (Question 1 of 1)</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-fig\">🖼️ Figure / Diagram</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-loc\">UNIVERSAL: ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-subj\">SUB_MED_PSM — Community Medicine (Preventive &amp; Social Medicine)</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-diff medium\">Medium</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-pyq\">PYQ: Standard Science Curriculum Figure Assessment (2023)</span><span class=\"badge b-meta\">Body: Open Science Curriculum · ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"q-meta\">\n          <span><strong>Domain:</strong> Medical &amp; Health Sciences</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Unit:</strong> Basic economic principles</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Topic:</strong> economics</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      \n        <div class=\"passage-box\">\n          <div class=\"passage-hdr\">\n            <span>📖 Context Passage — Economics</span>\n            <span class=\"passage-tag\">Shared ID: PSG_F78B53FD94DB · Question 1 of 1</span>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"passage-body\"><p>Trade happens when people agree to exchange goods and services. People give up something to get something else. Sometimes people barter, or directly exchange one good or service for another.\nMonica and Troy open their lunch boxes in the school cafeteria. Both of them could be happier with their lunches. Monica wanted broccoli in her lunch and Troy was hoping for tomatoes. Look at the images of their lunches. Then answer the question below.</p></div>\n          \n        </div>\n        \n      <div class=\"q-stem\"><p>What can Monica and Troy trade to each get what they want?</p></div>\n      <div class=\"q-diagram-box\"><img class=\"q-figure-img\" src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Official Exam Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n      <div class=\"opts-grid\"><div class=\"opt-item correct-opt\"><span class=\"opt-letter\">A</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Monica can trade her tomatoes for Troy&#x27;s broccoli.</div><span class=\"corr-tag\">✓ Correct</span></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">B</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Troy can trade his almonds for Monica&#x27;s tomatoes.</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">C</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Troy can trade his broccoli for Monica&#x27;s oranges.</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">D</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Monica can trade her tomatoes for Troy&#x27;s sandwich.</div></div></div>\n      <details class=\"expl-box\">\n        <summary>View Explanation & Provenance (Answer: Option A)</summary>\n        <div class=\"expl-body\">\n          <p>Monica wanted broccoli in her lunch and Troy was hoping for tomatoes. Look at the labeled part of the images.\nMonica has tomatoes. Troy has broccoli. They can trade tomatoes for broccoli to both be happier. Trading other things would not help either person get more items they want.</p>\n          \n          <div class=\"hash-row\">\n            <span><strong>Source/License:</strong> ScienceQA Open Figure Benchmark</span>\n            <span><strong>SHA-256:</strong> <code>2f9f651571a999a0f5b8...</code></span>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </details>\n    </article>\n    \n    <article class=\"q-card\">\n      <div class=\"q-head\">\n        <div class=\"q-badges\">\n          <span class=\"badge b-id\">#83 · SUBSCIPHYS_Q494694</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-db\">DB1_SCRAPED_MODIFIED</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-psg\">📖 Passage PSG_F7728CA7AA00 (Question 1 of 1)</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-fig\">🖼️ Figure / Diagram</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-loc\">UNIVERSAL: ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-subj\">SUB_SCI_PHYSICS — General Science (Physics, Chemistry, Biology &amp; Geography)</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-diff medium\">Medium</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-pyq\">PYQ: Standard Science Curriculum Figure Assessment (2023)</span><span class=\"badge b-meta\">Body: Open Science Curriculum · ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"q-meta\">\n          <span><strong>Domain:</strong> Science</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Unit:</strong> Plate tectonics</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Topic:</strong> earth-science</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      \n        <div class=\"passage-box\">\n          <div class=\"passage-hdr\">\n            <span>📖 Context Passage — Earth Science</span>\n            <span class=\"passage-tag\">Shared ID: PSG_F7728CA7AA00 · Question 1 of 1</span>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"passage-body\"><p>Read the passage and look at the picture.\nThe Aden Ridge began to form millions of years ago as the Somalian Plate and the Arabian Plate moved away from each other. The plates are slowly moving apart at a rate of about 2 centimeters per year. On the map, the ridge is shown in the Gulf of Aden, between the countries of Somalia and Yemen.</p></div>\n          \n        </div>\n        \n      <div class=\"q-stem\"><p>Complete the sentence.\nThe Aden Ridge formed at a () boundary.</p></div>\n      <div class=\"q-diagram-box\"><img class=\"q-figure-img\" src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Official Exam Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n      <div class=\"opts-grid\"><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">A</span><div class=\"opt-text\">convergent</div></div><div class=\"opt-item correct-opt\"><span class=\"opt-letter\">B</span><div class=\"opt-text\">divergent</div><span class=\"corr-tag\">✓ Correct</span></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">C</span><div class=\"opt-text\">transform</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">D</span><div class=\"opt-text\">None of the above</div></div></div>\n      <details class=\"expl-box\">\n        <summary>View Explanation & Provenance (Answer: Option B)</summary>\n        <div class=\"expl-body\">\n          <p>The outer layer of Earth is broken up into many pieces called tectonic plates, or simply plates. The breaks between plates are called plate boundaries. Plate boundaries are classified by the way the plates are moving relative to each other:\nAt a transform boundary, two plates are sliding past each other.\nAt a convergent boundary, two plates are moving toward each other.\nAt a divergent boundary, two plates are moving away from each other.\ndivergent plate boundary\nWhen plates at a divergent boundary move apart, cracks form in the crust along the boundary. Melted rock rises from below the crust to fill these cracks. As the melted rock cools and hardens, it becomes new oceanic crust.\nNewer oceanic crust weighs less than older oceanic crust. So, the crust on either side of the boundary rises up higher than the older crust that is farther from the boundary. This difference in elevation creates a mid-ocean ridge, or underwater mountain chain. Between the two plates, there may be a deep rift valley. To figure out what type of plate boundary formed the Aden Ridge, you need to know how the tectonic plates interacted. To find this out, read the passage carefully.\nThe Aden Ridge began to form millions of years ago as the Somalian Plate and the Arabian Plate moved away from each other. The plates are slowly moving apart at a rate of about 2 centimeters per year. On the map, the ridge is shown in the Gulf of Aden, between the countries of Somalia and Yemen.\nThe underlined part of the passage explains that the Aden Ridge formed as the two plates moved away from each other, or diverged. So, the Aden Ridge formed at a divergent boundary.</p>\n          \n          <div class=\"hash-row\">\n            <span><strong>Source/License:</strong> ScienceQA Open Figure Benchmark</span>\n            <span><strong>SHA-256:</strong> <code>443531f452a0e721e5e5...</code></span>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </details>\n    </article>\n    \n    <article class=\"q-card\">\n      <div class=\"q-head\">\n        <div class=\"q-badges\">\n          <span class=\"badge b-id\">#84 · SUBSCIBIOL_Q493959</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-db\">DB1_SCRAPED_MODIFIED</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-psg\">📖 Passage PSG_F7638AA3B688 (Question 1 of 1)</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-loc\">UNIVERSAL: ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-subj\">SUB_SCI_BIOLOGY — Life Sciences &amp; Biology (Botany, Zoology, Cell Biology, Genetics &amp; Ecology)</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-diff medium\">Medium</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-pyq\">PYQ: Standard Science Curriculum Figure Assessment (2023)</span><span class=\"badge b-meta\">Body: Open Science Curriculum · ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"q-meta\">\n          <span><strong>Domain:</strong> Science</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Unit:</strong> Genes to traits</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Topic:</strong> biology</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      \n        <div class=\"passage-box\">\n          <div class=\"passage-hdr\">\n            <span>📖 Context Passage — Biology</span>\n            <span class=\"passage-tag\">Shared ID: PSG_F7638AA3B688 · Question 1 of 1</span>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"passage-body\"><p>Read the description of a trait.\nMark knows how to do tricks with a jump rope.</p></div>\n          \n        </div>\n        \n      <div class=\"q-stem\"><p>What information supports the conclusion that Mark acquired this trait?</p></div>\n      \n      <div class=\"opts-grid\"><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">A</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Mark won a competition at his school with his jump rope tricks.</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">B</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Mark has three jump ropes, each made of a different material.</div></div><div class=\"opt-item correct-opt\"><span class=\"opt-letter\">C</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Mark&#x27;s sister taught him how to do tricks with a jump rope.</div><span class=\"corr-tag\">✓ Correct</span></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">D</span><div class=\"opt-text\">None of the above</div></div></div>\n      <details class=\"expl-box\">\n        <summary>View Explanation & Provenance (Answer: Option C)</summary>\n        <div class=\"expl-body\">\n          <p>Organisms, including people, have both inherited and acquired traits. Inherited and acquired traits are gained in different ways.\nInherited traits are passed down from biological parents to their offspring through genes. Genes are pieces of hereditary material that contain the instructions that affect inherited traits. Offspring receive their genes, and therefore gain their inherited traits, from their biological parents. Inherited traits do not need to be learned.\nAcquired traits are gained during a person&#x27;s life. Some acquired traits, such as riding a bicycle, are gained by learning. Other acquired traits, such as scars, are caused by the environment. Parents do not pass acquired traits down to their offspring.</p>\n          \n          <div class=\"hash-row\">\n            <span><strong>Source/License:</strong> ScienceQA Open Figure Benchmark</span>\n            <span><strong>SHA-256:</strong> <code>fc0efa1c32caa8bc405f...</code></span>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </details>\n    </article>\n    \n    <article class=\"q-card\">\n      <div class=\"q-head\">\n        <div class=\"q-badges\">\n          <span class=\"badge b-id\">#85 · SUBMEDPSM_Q493916</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-db\">DB1_SCRAPED_MODIFIED</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-psg\">📖 Passage PSG_F75D518D6D1D (Question 1 of 1)</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-fig\">🖼️ Figure / Diagram</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-loc\">UNIVERSAL: ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-subj\">SUB_MED_PSM — Community Medicine (Preventive &amp; Social Medicine)</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-diff medium\">Medium</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-pyq\">PYQ: Standard Science Curriculum Figure Assessment (2023)</span><span class=\"badge b-meta\">Body: Open Science Curriculum · ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"q-meta\">\n          <span><strong>Domain:</strong> Medical &amp; Health Sciences</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Unit:</strong> Basic economic principles</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Topic:</strong> economics</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      \n        <div class=\"passage-box\">\n          <div class=\"passage-hdr\">\n            <span>📖 Context Passage — Economics</span>\n            <span class=\"passage-tag\">Shared ID: PSG_F75D518D6D1D · Question 1 of 1</span>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"passage-body\"><p>Trade happens when people agree to exchange goods and services. People give up something to get something else. Sometimes people barter, or directly exchange one good or service for another.\nFelipe and Kevin open their lunch boxes in the school cafeteria. Both of them could be happier with their lunches. Felipe wanted broccoli in his lunch and Kevin was hoping for tomatoes. Look at the images of their lunches. Then answer the question below.</p></div>\n          \n        </div>\n        \n      <div class=\"q-stem\"><p>What can Felipe and Kevin trade to each get what they want?</p></div>\n      <div class=\"q-diagram-box\"><img class=\"q-figure-img\" src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Official Exam Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n      <div class=\"opts-grid\"><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">A</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Felipe can trade his tomatoes for Kevin&#x27;s sandwich.</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">B</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Kevin can trade his almonds for Felipe&#x27;s tomatoes.</div></div><div class=\"opt-item correct-opt\"><span class=\"opt-letter\">C</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Felipe can trade his tomatoes for Kevin&#x27;s broccoli.</div><span class=\"corr-tag\">✓ Correct</span></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">D</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Kevin can trade his broccoli for Felipe&#x27;s oranges.</div></div></div>\n      <details class=\"expl-box\">\n        <summary>View Explanation & Provenance (Answer: Option C)</summary>\n        <div class=\"expl-body\">\n          <p>Felipe wanted broccoli in his lunch and Kevin was hoping for tomatoes. Look at the labeled part of the images.\nFelipe has tomatoes. Kevin has broccoli. They can trade tomatoes for broccoli to both be happier. Trading other things would not help either person get more items they want.</p>\n          \n          <div class=\"hash-row\">\n            <span><strong>Source/License:</strong> ScienceQA Open Figure Benchmark</span>\n            <span><strong>SHA-256:</strong> <code>ca4437c4d894727224a5...</code></span>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </details>\n    </article>\n    \n    <article class=\"q-card\">\n      <div class=\"q-head\">\n        <div class=\"q-badges\">\n          <span class=\"badge b-id\">#86 · SUBSCIBIOL_Q492994</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-db\">DB1_SCRAPED_MODIFIED</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-psg\">📖 Passage PSG_F72E98BDA222 (Question 1 of 1)</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-loc\">UNIVERSAL: ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-subj\">SUB_SCI_BIOLOGY — Life Sciences &amp; Biology (Botany, Zoology, Cell Biology, Genetics &amp; Ecology)</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-diff medium\">Medium</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-pyq\">PYQ: Standard Science Curriculum Figure Assessment (2023)</span><span class=\"badge b-meta\">Body: Open Science Curriculum · ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"q-meta\">\n          <span><strong>Domain:</strong> Science</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Unit:</strong> Genes to traits</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Topic:</strong> biology</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      \n        <div class=\"passage-box\">\n          <div class=\"passage-hdr\">\n            <span>📖 Context Passage — Biology</span>\n            <span class=\"passage-tag\">Shared ID: PSG_F72E98BDA222 · Question 1 of 1</span>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"passage-body\"><p>Read the description of a trait.\nAva knows how to make chili.</p></div>\n          \n        </div>\n        \n      <div class=\"q-stem\"><p>What information supports the conclusion that Ava acquired this trait?</p></div>\n      \n      <div class=\"opts-grid\"><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">A</span><div class=\"opt-text\">When Ava was young, her grandmother taught her how to cut chili peppers.</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">B</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Ava&#x27;s friends like to make chili with her.</div></div><div class=\"opt-item correct-opt\"><span class=\"opt-letter\">C</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Ava learned how to make chili from a recipe book.</div><span class=\"corr-tag\">✓ Correct</span></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">D</span><div class=\"opt-text\">None of the above</div></div></div>\n      <details class=\"expl-box\">\n        <summary>View Explanation & Provenance (Answer: Option C)</summary>\n        <div class=\"expl-body\">\n          <p>Organisms, including people, have both inherited and acquired traits. Inherited and acquired traits are gained in different ways.\nInherited traits are passed down from biological parents to their offspring through genes. Genes are pieces of hereditary material that contain the instructions that affect inherited traits. Offspring receive their genes, and therefore gain their inherited traits, from their biological parents. Inherited traits do not need to be learned.\nAcquired traits are gained during a person&#x27;s life. Some acquired traits, such as riding a bicycle, are gained by learning. Other acquired traits, such as scars, are caused by the environment. Parents do not pass acquired traits down to their offspring.</p>\n          \n          <div class=\"hash-row\">\n            <span><strong>Source/License:</strong> ScienceQA Open Figure Benchmark</span>\n            <span><strong>SHA-256:</strong> <code>1ec985e77490e0dea451...</code></span>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </details>\n    </article>\n    \n    <article class=\"q-card\">\n      <div class=\"q-head\">\n        <div class=\"q-badges\">\n          <span class=\"badge b-id\">#87 · SUBMEDENT_Q492677</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-db\">DB1_SCRAPED_MODIFIED</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-psg\">📖 Passage PSG_F70F4ECA1300 (Question 1 of 1)</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-loc\">UNIVERSAL: ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-subj\">SUB_MED_ENT — Otorhinolaryngology (ENT)</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-diff medium\">Medium</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-pyq\">PYQ: Standard Science Curriculum Figure Assessment (2023)</span><span class=\"badge b-meta\">Body: Open Science Curriculum · ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"q-meta\">\n          <span><strong>Domain:</strong> Medical &amp; Health Sciences</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Unit:</strong> Designing experiments</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Topic:</strong> science-and-engineering-practices</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      \n        <div class=\"passage-box\">\n          <div class=\"passage-hdr\">\n            <span>📖 Context Passage — Science And Engineering Practices</span>\n            <span class=\"passage-tag\">Shared ID: PSG_F70F4ECA1300 · Question 1 of 1</span>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"passage-body\"><p>Bridgette gets a basketball for her birthday and dribbles it around her neighborhood. She notices that sometimes the ball bounces higher than other times. She wonders what factors affect how high her ball bounces. So, she decides to design an experiment. She has the following supplies available:\none basketball\naccess to a brick patio\naccess to a grassy lawn\naccess to a gravel driveway\na meterstick</p></div>\n          \n        </div>\n        \n      <div class=\"q-stem\"><p>Using only these supplies, which question can Bridgette investigate with an experiment?</p></div>\n      \n      <div class=\"opts-grid\"><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">A</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Does the basketball bounce higher on a lawn or on a dirt path?</div></div><div class=\"opt-item correct-opt\"><span class=\"opt-letter\">B</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Does the basketball bounce higher on gravel or on grass?</div><span class=\"corr-tag\">✓ Correct</span></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">C</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Do larger basketballs bounce higher than smaller basketballs on a brick patio?</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">D</span><div class=\"opt-text\">None of the above</div></div></div>\n      <details class=\"expl-box\">\n        <summary>View Explanation & Provenance (Answer: Option B)</summary>\n        <div class=\"expl-body\">\n          <p>Experiments can be designed to answer specific questions. When designing an experiment, you must identify the supplies that are necessary to answer your question. In order to do this, you need to figure out what will be tested and what will be measured during the experiment.\nImagine that you are wondering if plants grow to different heights when planted in different types of soil. How might you decide what supplies are necessary to conduct this experiment?\nFirst, you need to identify the part of the experiment that will be tested, which is the independent variable. This is usually the part of the experiment that is different or changed. In this case, you would like to know how plants grow in different types of soil. So, you must have different types of soil available.\nNext, you need to identify the part of the experiment that will be measured or observed, which is the dependent variable. In this experiment, you would like to know if some plants grow taller than others. So, you must be able to compare the plants&#x27; heights. To do this, you can observe which plants are taller by looking at them, or you can measure their exact heights with a meterstick.\nSo, if you have different types of soil and can observe or measure the heights of your plants, then you have the supplies you need to investigate your question with an experiment!</p>\n          \n          <div class=\"hash-row\">\n            <span><strong>Source/License:</strong> ScienceQA Open Figure Benchmark</span>\n            <span><strong>SHA-256:</strong> <code>09b54164f43e42a82cea...</code></span>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </details>\n    </article>\n    \n    <article class=\"q-card\">\n      <div class=\"q-head\">\n        <div class=\"q-badges\">\n          <span class=\"badge b-id\">#88 · SUBLANGENG_Q496155</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-pyq-db\">DB2_PYQ</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-psg\">📖 Passage PSG_F7008A5CEA11 (Question 1 of 1)</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-loc\">UNIVERSAL: ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-subj\">SUB_LANG_ENGLISH — English Language, Reading Comprehension &amp; Verbal Ability</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-diff hard\">Hard</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-pyq\">PYQ: National Standardized English Reading Comprehension Examination (2018-2023)</span><span class=\"badge b-meta\">Body: Official Standardized Examination Board · ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"q-meta\">\n          <span><strong>Domain:</strong> Aptitude &amp; Comprehension</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Unit:</strong> Multi-Question Exam Reading Comprehension Passages</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Topic:</strong> Senior Competitive Exam Comprehension</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      \n        <div class=\"passage-box\">\n          <div class=\"passage-hdr\">\n            <span>📖 Shared Reading Comprehension Passage (high9216)</span>\n            <span class=\"passage-tag\">Shared ID: PSG_F7008A5CEA11 · Question 1 of 1</span>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"passage-body\"><p>Do you love holidays but hate gaining weight? You are not alone. Holidays are times for celebrating. Many people are worried about their weight. With proper planning, though, it is possible to keep normal weight during the holidays. The idea is to enjoy the holidays but not to eat too much. You don&#x27;t have to turn away from the foods that you enjoy.</p><p>Here are some tips for preventing weight gain and maintaining physical fitness:</p><p>Don&#x27;t skip meals. Before you leave home, have a small, low-fat meal or snack . This may help to avoid getting too excited before delicious foods.</p><p>Control the amount of food. Use a small plate that may encourage you to &quot;load up  &quot;. You should be most comfortable eating an amount of food about the size of your fist  .</p><p>Begin with soup and fruit or vegetables. Fill up beforehand on water-based soup and raw fruit or vegetables, or drink a large glass of water before you eat to help you to feel full.</p><p>Avoid high-fat foods. Dishes that look oily or creamy may have large amount of fat. Choose lean meat . Fill your plate with salad and green vegetables. Use lemon juice instead of creamy food.</p><p>Stick to physical activity. Don&#x27;t let exercise take a break during the holidays. A 20-minute walk helps to burn off extra calories.(238 words)</p></div>\n          \n        </div>\n        \n      <div class=\"q-stem\"><p>What is the best title of the passage?</p></div>\n      \n      <div class=\"opts-grid\"><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">A</span><div class=\"opt-text\">How to avoid holiday feasting.</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">B</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Do&#x27;s and don&#x27;ts for keeping slim and fit.</div></div><div class=\"opt-item correct-opt\"><span class=\"opt-letter\">C</span><div class=\"opt-text\">How to avoid weight gain over holidays.</div><span class=\"corr-tag\">✓ Correct</span></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">D</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Wonderful holidays, boring experiences.</div></div></div>\n      <details class=\"expl-box\">\n        <summary>View Explanation & Provenance (Answer: Option C)</summary>\n        <div class=\"expl-body\">\n          <p>The passage focuses on maintaining normal weight during holidays through planning, meal habits, and portion control. Therefore, its best title concerns avoiding weight gain over holidays. Option A is too narrow because feasting is only one concern, B is overly generic, and D is unrelated to the passage.</p>\n          \n          <div class=\"hash-row\">\n            <span><strong>Source/License:</strong> RACE Official Shared-Passage Exam PYQ (high9216) · AI_AUDITED_VERIFIED (g4f:)</span>\n            <span><strong>SHA-256:</strong> <code>d6f4aa202394d1429915...</code></span>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </details>\n    </article>\n    \n    <article class=\"q-card\">\n      <div class=\"q-head\">\n        <div class=\"q-badges\">\n          <span class=\"badge b-id\">#89 · SUBMEDPSM_Q492810</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-db\">DB1_SCRAPED_MODIFIED</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-psg\">📖 Passage PSG_F6F61CDF2C84 (Question 1 of 1)</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-fig\">🖼️ Figure / Diagram</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-loc\">UNIVERSAL: ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-subj\">SUB_MED_PSM — Community Medicine (Preventive &amp; Social Medicine)</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-diff medium\">Medium</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-pyq\">PYQ: Standard Science Curriculum Figure Assessment (2023)</span><span class=\"badge b-meta\">Body: Open Science Curriculum · ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"q-meta\">\n          <span><strong>Domain:</strong> Medical &amp; Health Sciences</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Unit:</strong> Basic economic principles</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Topic:</strong> economics</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      \n        <div class=\"passage-box\">\n          <div class=\"passage-hdr\">\n            <span>📖 Context Passage — Economics</span>\n            <span class=\"passage-tag\">Shared ID: PSG_F6F61CDF2C84 · Question 1 of 1</span>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"passage-body\"><p>Trade happens when people agree to exchange goods and services. People give up something to get something else. Sometimes people barter, or directly exchange one good or service for another.\nTanvi and Jeremiah open their lunch boxes in the school cafeteria. Neither Tanvi nor Jeremiah got everything that they wanted. The table below shows which items they each wanted:\n\nLook at the images of their lunches. Then answer the question below.\nTanvi&#x27;s lunch Jeremiah&#x27;s lunch</p></div>\n          \n        </div>\n        \n      <div class=\"q-stem\"><p>What can Tanvi and Jeremiah trade to each get what they want?</p></div>\n      <div class=\"q-diagram-box\"><img class=\"q-figure-img\" src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Official Exam Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n      <div class=\"opts-grid\"><div class=\"opt-item correct-opt\"><span class=\"opt-letter\">A</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Tanvi can trade her tomatoes for Jeremiah&#x27;s broccoli.</div><span class=\"corr-tag\">✓ Correct</span></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">B</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Jeremiah can trade his broccoli for Tanvi&#x27;s oranges.</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">C</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Jeremiah can trade his almonds for Tanvi&#x27;s tomatoes.</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">D</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Tanvi can trade her tomatoes for Jeremiah&#x27;s carrots.</div></div></div>\n      <details class=\"expl-box\">\n        <summary>View Explanation & Provenance (Answer: Option A)</summary>\n        <div class=\"expl-body\">\n          <p>Look at the table and images.\nTanvi wants broccoli. Jeremiah wants tomatoes. They can trade tomatoes for broccoli to both get what they want. Trading other things would not help both people get more items they want.</p>\n          \n          <div class=\"hash-row\">\n            <span><strong>Source/License:</strong> ScienceQA Open Figure Benchmark</span>\n            <span><strong>SHA-256:</strong> <code>15ab941ba2c14b541ce4...</code></span>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </details>\n    </article>\n    \n    <article class=\"q-card\">\n      <div class=\"q-head\">\n        <div class=\"q-badges\">\n          <span class=\"badge b-id\">#90 · SUBSCIBIOL_Q491310</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-db\">DB1_SCRAPED_MODIFIED</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-psg\">📖 Passage PSG_F6ED4F1B55CD (Question 1 of 1)</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-loc\">UNIVERSAL: ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-subj\">SUB_SCI_BIOLOGY — Life Sciences &amp; Biology (Botany, Zoology, Cell Biology, Genetics &amp; Ecology)</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-diff medium\">Medium</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-pyq\">PYQ: Standard Science Curriculum Figure Assessment (2023)</span><span class=\"badge b-meta\">Body: Open Science Curriculum · ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"q-meta\">\n          <span><strong>Domain:</strong> Science</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Unit:</strong> Genes to traits</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Topic:</strong> biology</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      \n        <div class=\"passage-box\">\n          <div class=\"passage-hdr\">\n            <span>📖 Context Passage — Biology</span>\n            <span class=\"passage-tag\">Shared ID: PSG_F6ED4F1B55CD · Question 1 of 1</span>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"passage-body\"><p>Read the description of a trait.\nTanvi knows how to fly a plane.</p></div>\n          \n        </div>\n        \n      <div class=\"q-stem\"><p>What information supports the conclusion that Tanvi acquired this trait?</p></div>\n      \n      <div class=\"opts-grid\"><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">A</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Tanvi can fly a plane on cloudy days and at night.</div></div><div class=\"opt-item correct-opt\"><span class=\"opt-letter\">B</span><div class=\"opt-text\">A pilot taught Tanvi how to fly a plane.</div><span class=\"corr-tag\">✓ Correct</span></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">C</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Tanvi is in the Air Force. She flies a plane almost every day.</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">D</span><div class=\"opt-text\">None of the above</div></div></div>\n      <details class=\"expl-box\">\n        <summary>View Explanation & Provenance (Answer: Option B)</summary>\n        <div class=\"expl-body\">\n          <p>Organisms, including people, have both inherited and acquired traits. Inherited and acquired traits are gained in different ways.\nInherited traits are passed down from biological parents to their offspring through genes. Genes are pieces of hereditary material that contain the instructions that affect inherited traits. Offspring receive their genes, and therefore gain their inherited traits, from their biological parents. Inherited traits do not need to be learned.\nAcquired traits are gained during a person&#x27;s life. Some acquired traits, such as riding a bicycle, are gained by learning. Other acquired traits, such as scars, are caused by the environment. Parents do not pass acquired traits down to their offspring.</p>\n          \n          <div class=\"hash-row\">\n            <span><strong>Source/License:</strong> ScienceQA Open Figure Benchmark</span>\n            <span><strong>SHA-256:</strong> <code>dd3fb7ce43b5a140f60e...</code></span>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </details>\n    </article>\n    \n    <article class=\"q-card\">\n      <div class=\"q-head\">\n        <div class=\"q-badges\">\n          <span class=\"badge b-id\">#91 · SUBMEDENT_Q490497</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-db\">DB1_SCRAPED_MODIFIED</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-psg\">📖 Passage PSG_F6CE40089AED (Question 1 of 1)</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-loc\">UNIVERSAL: ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-subj\">SUB_MED_ENT — Otorhinolaryngology (ENT)</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-diff medium\">Medium</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-pyq\">PYQ: Standard Science Curriculum Figure Assessment (2023)</span><span class=\"badge b-meta\">Body: Open Science Curriculum · ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"q-meta\">\n          <span><strong>Domain:</strong> Medical &amp; Health Sciences</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Unit:</strong> Designing experiments</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Topic:</strong> science-and-engineering-practices</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      \n        <div class=\"passage-box\">\n          <div class=\"passage-hdr\">\n            <span>📖 Context Passage — Science And Engineering Practices</span>\n            <span class=\"passage-tag\">Shared ID: PSG_F6CE40089AED · Question 1 of 1</span>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"passage-body\"><p>Whitney has a small vegetable garden, which includes a compost pile of food scraps. She notices that some of the squash plants growing next to the compost pile grow differently than squash plants that are farther away. She wonders what factors affect how her squash plants grow. So, she decides to design an experiment. She has the following supplies available:\none type of squash seeds\nfour large clay pots\nsoil\na compost pile\nwater</p></div>\n          \n        </div>\n        \n      <div class=\"q-stem\"><p>Using only these supplies, which question can Whitney investigate with an experiment?</p></div>\n      \n      <div class=\"opts-grid\"><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">A</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Do squash plants grow larger if the seeds are planted in small pots or in large pots?</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">B</span><div class=\"opt-text\">If squash seeds and tomato seeds are planted with compost, which type of plant grows larger?</div></div><div class=\"opt-item correct-opt\"><span class=\"opt-letter\">C</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Do squash plants grow larger if the seeds are planted with compost or without compost?</div><span class=\"corr-tag\">✓ Correct</span></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">D</span><div class=\"opt-text\">None of the above</div></div></div>\n      <details class=\"expl-box\">\n        <summary>View Explanation & Provenance (Answer: Option C)</summary>\n        <div class=\"expl-body\">\n          <p>Experiments can be designed to answer specific questions. When designing an experiment, you must identify the supplies that are necessary to answer your question. In order to do this, you need to figure out what will be tested and what will be measured during the experiment.\nImagine that you are wondering if plants grow to different heights when planted in different types of soil. How might you decide what supplies are necessary to conduct this experiment?\nFirst, you need to identify the part of the experiment that will be tested, which is the independent variable. This is usually the part of the experiment that is different or changed. In this case, you would like to know how plants grow in different types of soil. So, you must have different types of soil available.\nNext, you need to identify the part of the experiment that will be measured or observed, which is the dependent variable. In this experiment, you would like to know if some plants grow taller than others. So, you must be able to compare the plants&#x27; heights. To do this, you can observe which plants are taller by looking at them, or you can measure their exact heights with a meterstick.\nSo, if you have different types of soil and can observe or measure the heights of your plants, then you have the supplies you need to investigate your question with an experiment!</p>\n          \n          <div class=\"hash-row\">\n            <span><strong>Source/License:</strong> ScienceQA Open Figure Benchmark</span>\n            <span><strong>SHA-256:</strong> <code>226c1d8f002d4c90f58e...</code></span>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </details>\n    </article>\n    \n    <article class=\"q-card\">\n      <div class=\"q-head\">\n        <div class=\"q-badges\">\n          <span class=\"badge b-id\">#92 · SUBSCIBIOL_Q495371</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-db\">DB1_SCRAPED_MODIFIED</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-psg\">📖 Passage PSG_F68AB3162F3B (Question 1 of 1)</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-fig\">🖼️ Figure / Diagram</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-loc\">UNIVERSAL: ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-subj\">SUB_SCI_BIOLOGY — Life Sciences &amp; Biology (Botany, Zoology, Cell Biology, Genetics &amp; Ecology)</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-diff medium\">Medium</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-pyq\">PYQ: Standard Science Curriculum Figure Assessment (2023)</span><span class=\"badge b-meta\">Body: Open Science Curriculum · ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"q-meta\">\n          <span><strong>Domain:</strong> Science</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Unit:</strong> Plants</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Topic:</strong> biology</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      \n        <div class=\"passage-box\">\n          <div class=\"passage-hdr\">\n            <span>📖 Context Passage — Biology</span>\n            <span class=\"passage-tag\">Shared ID: PSG_F68AB3162F3B · Question 1 of 1</span>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"passage-body\"><p>People use carrot plants for food. We usually eat the part of this plant that takes in water and nutrients. It holds the plant in place in the soil.</p></div>\n          \n        </div>\n        \n      <div class=\"q-stem\"><p>Which part of the carrot plant do we usually eat?</p></div>\n      <div class=\"q-diagram-box\"><img class=\"q-figure-img\" src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Official Exam Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n      <div class=\"opts-grid\"><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">A</span><div class=\"opt-text\">the fruit</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">B</span><div class=\"opt-text\">the stem</div></div><div class=\"opt-item correct-opt\"><span class=\"opt-letter\">C</span><div class=\"opt-text\">the root</div><span class=\"corr-tag\">✓ Correct</span></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">D</span><div class=\"opt-text\">None of the above</div></div></div>\n      <details class=\"expl-box\">\n        <summary>View Explanation & Provenance (Answer: Option C)</summary>\n        <div class=\"expl-body\">\n          <p>The fruits and vegetables we eat are parts of plants! Plants are made up of different structures. The different structures carry out important functions.\nThe roots take in water and nutrients from the soil. They also hold the plant in place in the soil.\nThe stem supports the plant. It carries food, water, and nutrients through the plant.\nThe leaves are where most of the plant&#x27;s photosynthesis happens. Photosynthesis is the process plants use to turn water, sunlight, and carbon dioxide into food.\nAfter they are pollinated, the flowers make seeds and fruit.\nThe fruit contain the seeds. Each fruit grows from a pollinated flower.\nThe seeds can grow into a new plant. Germination is when a seed begins to grow. The part of the carrot plant we usually eat is the root. It takes in water and nutrients. It also holds the plant in place in the soil.</p>\n          \n          <div class=\"hash-row\">\n            <span><strong>Source/License:</strong> ScienceQA Open Figure Benchmark</span>\n            <span><strong>SHA-256:</strong> <code>e06ae60014debe7c8a66...</code></span>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </details>\n    </article>\n    \n    <article class=\"q-card\">\n      <div class=\"q-head\">\n        <div class=\"q-badges\">\n          <span class=\"badge b-id\">#93 · SUBSCIBIOL_Q492990</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-db\">DB1_SCRAPED_MODIFIED</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-psg\">📖 Passage PSG_F6881A36A40A (Question 1 of 1)</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-loc\">UNIVERSAL: ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-subj\">SUB_SCI_BIOLOGY — Life Sciences &amp; Biology (Botany, Zoology, Cell Biology, Genetics &amp; Ecology)</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-diff medium\">Medium</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-pyq\">PYQ: Standard Science Curriculum Figure Assessment (2023)</span><span class=\"badge b-meta\">Body: Open Science Curriculum · ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"q-meta\">\n          <span><strong>Domain:</strong> Science</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Unit:</strong> Genes to traits</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Topic:</strong> biology</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      \n        <div class=\"passage-box\">\n          <div class=\"passage-hdr\">\n            <span>📖 Context Passage — Biology</span>\n            <span class=\"passage-tag\">Shared ID: PSG_F6881A36A40A · Question 1 of 1</span>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"passage-body\"><p>Read the description of a trait.\nLogan has wavy hair.</p></div>\n          \n        </div>\n        \n      <div class=\"q-stem\"><p>What information supports the conclusion that Logan inherited this trait?</p></div>\n      \n      <div class=\"opts-grid\"><div class=\"opt-item correct-opt\"><span class=\"opt-letter\">A</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Logan&#x27;s biological parents have wavy hair.</div><span class=\"corr-tag\">✓ Correct</span></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">B</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Logan&#x27;s biological mother has long hair. Logan also has long hair.</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">C</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Logan uses a headband to keep his wavy hair out of his face.</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">D</span><div class=\"opt-text\">None of the above</div></div></div>\n      <details class=\"expl-box\">\n        <summary>View Explanation & Provenance (Answer: Option A)</summary>\n        <div class=\"expl-body\">\n          <p>Organisms, including people, have both inherited and acquired traits. Inherited and acquired traits are gained in different ways.\nInherited traits are passed down from biological parents to their offspring through genes. Genes are pieces of hereditary material that contain the instructions that affect inherited traits. Offspring receive their genes, and therefore gain their inherited traits, from their biological parents. Inherited traits do not need to be learned.\nAcquired traits are gained during a person&#x27;s life. Some acquired traits, such as riding a bicycle, are gained by learning. Other acquired traits, such as scars, are caused by the environment. Parents do not pass acquired traits down to their offspring.</p>\n          \n          <div class=\"hash-row\">\n            <span><strong>Source/License:</strong> ScienceQA Open Figure Benchmark</span>\n            <span><strong>SHA-256:</strong> <code>5a827ba2baa3e500daa3...</code></span>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </details>\n    </article>\n    \n    <article class=\"q-card\">\n      <div class=\"q-head\">\n        <div class=\"q-badges\">\n          <span class=\"badge b-id\">#94 · SUBMEDENT_Q491855</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-db\">DB1_SCRAPED_MODIFIED</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-psg\">📖 Passage PSG_F6876EDC35A7 (Question 1 of 1)</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-loc\">UNIVERSAL: ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-subj\">SUB_MED_ENT — Otorhinolaryngology (ENT)</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-diff medium\">Medium</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-pyq\">PYQ: Standard Science Curriculum Figure Assessment (2023)</span><span class=\"badge b-meta\">Body: Open Science Curriculum · ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"q-meta\">\n          <span><strong>Domain:</strong> Medical &amp; Health Sciences</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Unit:</strong> Designing experiments</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Topic:</strong> science-and-engineering-practices</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      \n        <div class=\"passage-box\">\n          <div class=\"passage-hdr\">\n            <span>📖 Context Passage — Science And Engineering Practices</span>\n            <span class=\"passage-tag\">Shared ID: PSG_F6876EDC35A7 · Question 1 of 1</span>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"passage-body\"><p>After Logan cleans up a spill, he hangs a wet cloth towel in the laundry room. Two hours later, he notices that the towel has partially dried. He wonders what factors affect how cloth dries. So, he decides to design an experiment. He has the following supplies available:\ntwo identical white cloth towels\nwater\na clothesline in the laundry room\na clothesline in the backyard</p></div>\n          \n        </div>\n        \n      <div class=\"q-stem\"><p>Using only these supplies, which question can Logan investigate with an experiment?</p></div>\n      \n      <div class=\"opts-grid\"><div class=\"opt-item correct-opt\"><span class=\"opt-letter\">A</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Do cloth towels dry faster if they are hung in the laundry room or in the backyard?</div><span class=\"corr-tag\">✓ Correct</span></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">B</span><div class=\"opt-text\">When hung in the laundry room, do black cloth towels or white cloth towels dry more quickly?</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">C</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Does a small cloth towel or a large cloth towel dry faster when hung in the backyard?</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">D</span><div class=\"opt-text\">None of the above</div></div></div>\n      <details class=\"expl-box\">\n        <summary>View Explanation & Provenance (Answer: Option A)</summary>\n        <div class=\"expl-body\">\n          <p>Experiments can be designed to answer specific questions. When designing an experiment, you must identify the supplies that are necessary to answer your question. In order to do this, you need to figure out what will be tested and what will be measured during the experiment.\nImagine that you are wondering if plants grow to different heights when planted in different types of soil. How might you decide what supplies are necessary to conduct this experiment?\nFirst, you need to identify the part of the experiment that will be tested, which is the independent variable. This is usually the part of the experiment that is different or changed. In this case, you would like to know how plants grow in different types of soil. So, you must have different types of soil available.\nNext, you need to identify the part of the experiment that will be measured or observed, which is the dependent variable. In this experiment, you would like to know if some plants grow taller than others. So, you must be able to compare the plants&#x27; heights. To do this, you can observe which plants are taller by looking at them, or you can measure their exact heights with a meterstick.\nSo, if you have different types of soil and can observe or measure the heights of your plants, then you have the supplies you need to investigate your question with an experiment!</p>\n          \n          <div class=\"hash-row\">\n            <span><strong>Source/License:</strong> ScienceQA Open Figure Benchmark</span>\n            <span><strong>SHA-256:</strong> <code>bef28fdf7507d50b1f3d...</code></span>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </details>\n    </article>\n    \n    <article class=\"q-card\">\n      <div class=\"q-head\">\n        <div class=\"q-badges\">\n          <span class=\"badge b-id\">#95 · SUBSCIBIOL_Q492380</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-db\">DB1_SCRAPED_MODIFIED</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-psg\">📖 Passage PSG_F6864A169F9C (Question 1 of 2)</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-fig\">🖼️ Figure / Diagram</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-loc\">UNIVERSAL: ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-subj\">SUB_SCI_BIOLOGY — Life Sciences &amp; Biology (Botany, Zoology, Cell Biology, Genetics &amp; Ecology)</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-diff medium\">Medium</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-pyq\">PYQ: Standard Science Curriculum Figure Assessment (2023)</span><span class=\"badge b-meta\">Body: Open Science Curriculum · ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"q-meta\">\n          <span><strong>Domain:</strong> Science</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Unit:</strong> Ecosystems</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Topic:</strong> biology</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      \n        <div class=\"passage-box\">\n          <div class=\"passage-hdr\">\n            <span>📖 Context Passage — Biology</span>\n            <span class=\"passage-tag\">Shared ID: PSG_F6864A169F9C · Question 1 of 2</span>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"passage-body\"><p>Figure: Catoctin Mountain Park.\nCatoctin Mountain Park is a temperate deciduous forest ecosystem in Maryland. Most of this forest was cut down for its wood in the early 1900s. But since the 1940s, conservation efforts have allowed the forest to return to much of this park.</p></div>\n          \n        </div>\n        \n      <div class=\"q-stem\"><p>Which statement describes the Catoctin Mountain Park ecosystem?</p></div>\n      <div class=\"q-diagram-box\"><img class=\"q-figure-img\" src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Official Exam Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n      <div class=\"opts-grid\"><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">A</span><div class=\"opt-text\">It has soil that is poor in nutrients.</div></div><div class=\"opt-item correct-opt\"><span class=\"opt-letter\">B</span><div class=\"opt-text\">It has only a few types of trees.</div><span class=\"corr-tag\">✓ Correct</span></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">C</span><div class=\"opt-text\">It has many different types of trees.</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">D</span><div class=\"opt-text\">None of the above</div></div></div>\n      <details class=\"expl-box\">\n        <summary>View Explanation & Provenance (Answer: Option B)</summary>\n        <div class=\"expl-body\">\n          <p>An environment includes all of the biotic, or living, and abiotic, or nonliving, things in an area. An ecosystem is created by the relationships that form among the biotic and abiotic parts of an environment.\nThere are many different types of terrestrial, or land-based, ecosystems. Here are some ways in which terrestrial ecosystems can differ from each other:\nthe pattern of weather, or climate\nthe type of soil\nthe organisms that live there A temperate deciduous forest is a type of ecosystem. Temperate deciduous forests have the following features: warm, wet summers and cold, wet winters, soil that is rich in nutrients, and only a few types of trees. So, the following statement describes the Catoctin Mountain Park ecosystem: warm, wet summers and cold, wet winters, soil that is rich in nutrients, and only a few types of trees. It has only a few types of trees. The following statements do not describe Catoctin Mountain Park: warm, wet summers and cold, wet winters, soil that is rich in nutrients, and only a few types of trees. It has many different types of trees. It has soil that is poor in nutrients.</p>\n          \n          <div class=\"hash-row\">\n            <span><strong>Source/License:</strong> ScienceQA Open Figure Benchmark</span>\n            <span><strong>SHA-256:</strong> <code>15e0750391302186accf...</code></span>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </details>\n    </article>\n    \n    <article class=\"q-card\">\n      <div class=\"q-head\">\n        <div class=\"q-badges\">\n          <span class=\"badge b-id\">#96 · SUBSCIBIOL_Q492534</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-db\">DB1_SCRAPED_MODIFIED</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-psg\">📖 Passage PSG_F6864A169F9C (Question 2 of 2)</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-fig\">🖼️ Figure / Diagram</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-loc\">UNIVERSAL: ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-subj\">SUB_SCI_BIOLOGY — Life Sciences &amp; Biology (Botany, Zoology, Cell Biology, Genetics &amp; Ecology)</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-diff medium\">Medium</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-pyq\">PYQ: Standard Science Curriculum Figure Assessment (2023)</span><span class=\"badge b-meta\">Body: Open Science Curriculum · ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"q-meta\">\n          <span><strong>Domain:</strong> Science</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Unit:</strong> Ecosystems</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Topic:</strong> biology</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      \n        <div class=\"passage-box\">\n          <div class=\"passage-hdr\">\n            <span>📖 Context Passage — Biology</span>\n            <span class=\"passage-tag\">Shared ID: PSG_F6864A169F9C · Question 2 of 2</span>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"passage-body\"><p>Figure: Catoctin Mountain Park.\nCatoctin Mountain Park is a temperate deciduous forest ecosystem in Maryland. Most of this forest was cut down for its wood in the early 1900s. But since the 1940s, conservation efforts have allowed the forest to return to much of this park.</p></div>\n          \n        </div>\n        \n      <div class=\"q-stem\"><p>Which statement describes the Catoctin Mountain Park ecosystem?</p></div>\n      <div class=\"q-diagram-box\"><img class=\"q-figure-img\" src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Official Exam Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n      <div class=\"opts-grid\"><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">A</span><div class=\"opt-text\">It has many different types of trees.</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">B</span><div class=\"opt-text\">It has soil that is poor in nutrients.</div></div><div class=\"opt-item correct-opt\"><span class=\"opt-letter\">C</span><div class=\"opt-text\">It has soil that is rich in nutrients.</div><span class=\"corr-tag\">✓ Correct</span></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">D</span><div class=\"opt-text\">None of the above</div></div></div>\n      <details class=\"expl-box\">\n        <summary>View Explanation & Provenance (Answer: Option C)</summary>\n        <div class=\"expl-body\">\n          <p>An environment includes all of the biotic, or living, and abiotic, or nonliving, things in an area. An ecosystem is created by the relationships that form among the biotic and abiotic parts of an environment.\nThere are many different types of terrestrial, or land-based, ecosystems. Here are some ways in which terrestrial ecosystems can differ from each other:\nthe pattern of weather, or climate\nthe type of soil\nthe organisms that live there A temperate deciduous forest is a type of ecosystem. Temperate deciduous forests have the following features: warm, wet summers and cold, wet winters, soil that is rich in nutrients, and only a few types of trees. So, the following statement describes the Catoctin Mountain Park ecosystem: warm, wet summers and cold, wet winters, soil that is rich in nutrients, and only a few types of trees. It has soil that is rich in nutrients. The following statements do not describe Catoctin Mountain Park: warm, wet summers and cold, wet winters, soil that is rich in nutrients, and only a few types of trees. It has soil that is poor in nutrients. It has many different types of trees.</p>\n          \n          <div class=\"hash-row\">\n            <span><strong>Source/License:</strong> ScienceQA Open Figure Benchmark</span>\n            <span><strong>SHA-256:</strong> <code>0f06a95469956a2681b5...</code></span>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </details>\n    </article>\n    \n    <article class=\"q-card\">\n      <div class=\"q-head\">\n        <div class=\"q-badges\">\n          <span class=\"badge b-id\">#97 · SUBSCIBIOL_Q494860</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-db\">DB1_SCRAPED_MODIFIED</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-psg\">📖 Passage PSG_F6696A7383DE (Question 1 of 1)</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-loc\">UNIVERSAL: ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-subj\">SUB_SCI_BIOLOGY — Life Sciences &amp; Biology (Botany, Zoology, Cell Biology, Genetics &amp; Ecology)</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-diff medium\">Medium</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-pyq\">PYQ: Standard Science Curriculum Figure Assessment (2023)</span><span class=\"badge b-meta\">Body: Open Science Curriculum · ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"q-meta\">\n          <span><strong>Domain:</strong> Science</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Unit:</strong> Genes to traits</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Topic:</strong> biology</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      \n        <div class=\"passage-box\">\n          <div class=\"passage-hdr\">\n            <span>📖 Context Passage — Biology</span>\n            <span class=\"passage-tag\">Shared ID: PSG_F6696A7383DE · Question 1 of 1</span>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"passage-body\"><p>Read the description of a trait.\nNathan has wavy hair.</p></div>\n          \n        </div>\n        \n      <div class=\"q-stem\"><p>What information supports the conclusion that Nathan inherited this trait?</p></div>\n      \n      <div class=\"opts-grid\"><div class=\"opt-item correct-opt\"><span class=\"opt-letter\">A</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Nathan&#x27;s biological parents have wavy hair.</div><span class=\"corr-tag\">✓ Correct</span></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">B</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Nathan&#x27;s biological mother has long hair. Nathan also has long hair.</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">C</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Nathan uses a headband to keep his wavy hair out of his face.</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">D</span><div class=\"opt-text\">None of the above</div></div></div>\n      <details class=\"expl-box\">\n        <summary>View Explanation & Provenance (Answer: Option A)</summary>\n        <div class=\"expl-body\">\n          <p>Organisms, including people, have both inherited and acquired traits. Inherited and acquired traits are gained in different ways.\nInherited traits are passed down from biological parents to their offspring through genes. Genes are pieces of hereditary material that contain the instructions that affect inherited traits. Offspring receive their genes, and therefore gain their inherited traits, from their biological parents. Inherited traits do not need to be learned.\nAcquired traits are gained during a person&#x27;s life. Some acquired traits, such as riding a bicycle, are gained by learning. Other acquired traits, such as scars, are caused by the environment. Parents do not pass acquired traits down to their offspring.</p>\n          \n          <div class=\"hash-row\">\n            <span><strong>Source/License:</strong> ScienceQA Open Figure Benchmark</span>\n            <span><strong>SHA-256:</strong> <code>188e82b6bfdec27b349c...</code></span>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </details>\n    </article>\n    \n    <article class=\"q-card\">\n      <div class=\"q-head\">\n        <div class=\"q-badges\">\n          <span class=\"badge b-id\">#98 · SUBMEDPSM_Q492889</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-db\">DB1_SCRAPED_MODIFIED</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-psg\">📖 Passage PSG_F651168FE83F (Question 1 of 1)</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-fig\">🖼️ Figure / Diagram</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-loc\">UNIVERSAL: ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-subj\">SUB_MED_PSM — Community Medicine (Preventive &amp; Social Medicine)</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-diff medium\">Medium</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-pyq\">PYQ: Standard Science Curriculum Figure Assessment (2023)</span><span class=\"badge b-meta\">Body: Open Science Curriculum · ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"q-meta\">\n          <span><strong>Domain:</strong> Medical &amp; Health Sciences</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Unit:</strong> Basic economic principles</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Topic:</strong> economics</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      \n        <div class=\"passage-box\">\n          <div class=\"passage-hdr\">\n            <span>📖 Context Passage — Economics</span>\n            <span class=\"passage-tag\">Shared ID: PSG_F651168FE83F · Question 1 of 1</span>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"passage-body\"><p>Trade happens when people agree to exchange goods and services. People give up something to get something else. Sometimes people barter, or directly exchange one good or service for another.\nAusten and Kelly open their lunch boxes in the school cafeteria. Both of them could be happier with their lunches. Austen wanted broccoli in his lunch and Kelly was hoping for tomatoes. Look at the images of their lunches. Then answer the question below.</p></div>\n          \n        </div>\n        \n      <div class=\"q-stem\"><p>What can Austen and Kelly trade to each get what they want?</p></div>\n      <div class=\"q-diagram-box\"><img class=\"q-figure-img\" src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Official Exam Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n      <div class=\"opts-grid\"><div class=\"opt-item correct-opt\"><span class=\"opt-letter\">A</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Austen can trade his tomatoes for Kelly&#x27;s broccoli.</div><span class=\"corr-tag\">✓ Correct</span></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">B</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Kelly can trade her broccoli for Austen&#x27;s oranges.</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">C</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Kelly can trade her almonds for Austen&#x27;s tomatoes.</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">D</span><div class=\"opt-text\">Austen can trade his tomatoes for Kelly&#x27;s sandwich.</div></div></div>\n      <details class=\"expl-box\">\n        <summary>View Explanation & Provenance (Answer: Option A)</summary>\n        <div class=\"expl-body\">\n          <p>Austen wanted broccoli in his lunch and Kelly was hoping for tomatoes. Look at the labeled part of the images.\nAusten has tomatoes. Kelly has broccoli. They can trade tomatoes for broccoli to both be happier. Trading other things would not help either person get more items they want.</p>\n          \n          <div class=\"hash-row\">\n            <span><strong>Source/License:</strong> ScienceQA Open Figure Benchmark</span>\n            <span><strong>SHA-256:</strong> <code>8fcb988c4f929674f0dd...</code></span>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </details>\n    </article>\n    \n    <article class=\"q-card\">\n      <div class=\"q-head\">\n        <div class=\"q-badges\">\n          <span class=\"badge b-id\">#99 · SUBSCIPHYS_Q492239</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-db\">DB1_SCRAPED_MODIFIED</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-psg\">📖 Passage PSG_F63E7C6C445A (Question 1 of 1)</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-fig\">🖼️ Figure / Diagram</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-loc\">UNIVERSAL: ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-subj\">SUB_SCI_PHYSICS — General Science (Physics, Chemistry, Biology &amp; Geography)</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-diff medium\">Medium</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-pyq\">PYQ: Standard Science Curriculum Figure Assessment (2023)</span><span class=\"badge b-meta\">Body: Open Science Curriculum · ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"q-meta\">\n          <span><strong>Domain:</strong> Science</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Unit:</strong> Rocks and minerals</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Topic:</strong> earth-science</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      \n        <div class=\"passage-box\">\n          <div class=\"passage-hdr\">\n            <span>📖 Context Passage — Earth Science</span>\n            <span class=\"passage-tag\">Shared ID: PSG_F63E7C6C445A · Question 1 of 1</span>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"passage-body\"><p>This is a piece of gabbro. It formed through the cooling of melted rock below the earth&#x27;s surface, called magma.\nAs the magma cooled, mineral grains began to form. Gabbro is made of minerals such as olivine and feldspar. The mineral grains in gabbro are large. This is because the magma cooled slowly.</p></div>\n          \n        </div>\n        \n      <div class=\"q-stem\"><p>What type of rock is gabbro?</p></div>\n      <div class=\"q-diagram-box\"><img class=\"q-figure-img\" src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Official Exam Figure\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n      <div class=\"opts-grid\"><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">A</span><div class=\"opt-text\">sedimentary</div></div><div class=\"opt-item correct-opt\"><span class=\"opt-letter\">B</span><div class=\"opt-text\">igneous</div><span class=\"corr-tag\">✓ Correct</span></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">C</span><div class=\"opt-text\">metamorphic</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">D</span><div class=\"opt-text\">None of the above</div></div></div>\n      <details class=\"expl-box\">\n        <summary>View Explanation & Provenance (Answer: Option B)</summary>\n        <div class=\"expl-body\">\n          <p>Igneous rock is formed when melted rock cools and hardens into solid rock. This type of change can occur at Earth&#x27;s surface or below it.\nSedimentary rock is formed when layers of sediment are pressed together, or compacted, to make rock. This type of change occurs below Earth&#x27;s surface.\nMetamorphic rock is formed when a rock is changed by very high temperature and pressure. This type of change often occurs deep below Earth&#x27;s surface. Over time, the old rock becomes a new rock with different properties. Gabbro is an igneous rock. Like other igneous rocks, it forms when melted rock cools and hardens.\nMelted rock below the earth&#x27;s surface is called magma. Gabbro forms from a type of magma that is rich in iron and magnesium. As the magma cools, minerals such as olivine and feldspar begin to form. When the magma becomes solid, it turns into gabbro.</p>\n          \n          <div class=\"hash-row\">\n            <span><strong>Source/License:</strong> ScienceQA Open Figure Benchmark</span>\n            <span><strong>SHA-256:</strong> <code>279503f64fc4488cd5e5...</code></span>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </details>\n    </article>\n    \n    <article class=\"q-card\">\n      <div class=\"q-head\">\n        <div class=\"q-badges\">\n          <span class=\"badge b-id\">#100 · SUBMEDENT_Q493505</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-db\">DB1_SCRAPED_MODIFIED</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-psg\">📖 Passage PSG_F6165B2EDC33 (Question 1 of 1)</span>\n          \n          <span class=\"badge b-loc\">UNIVERSAL: ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-subj\">SUB_MED_ENT — Otorhinolaryngology (ENT)</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-diff medium\">Medium</span>\n          <span class=\"badge b-pyq\">PYQ: Standard Science Curriculum Figure Assessment (2023)</span><span class=\"badge b-meta\">Body: Open Science Curriculum · ALL_COUNTRIES</span>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"q-meta\">\n          <span><strong>Domain:</strong> Medical &amp; Health Sciences</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Unit:</strong> Kinetic and potential energy</span> ·\n          <span><strong>Topic:</strong> physics</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n      \n        <div class=\"passage-box\">\n          <div class=\"passage-hdr\">\n            <span>📖 Context Passage — Physics</span>\n            <span class=\"passage-tag\">Shared ID: PSG_F6165B2EDC33 · Question 1 of 1</span>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"passage-body\"><p>Read the text about a person in motion.\nAlec hiked up a tall mountain. He followed a trail all the way to the summit.</p></div>\n          \n        </div>\n        \n      <div class=\"q-stem\"><p>Complete the statement. Assume that Alec&#x27;s mass did not change.\nThe gravitational potential energy stored between Alec and Earth () as he hiked toward the summit.</p></div>\n      \n      <div class=\"opts-grid\"><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">A</span><div class=\"opt-text\">stayed the same</div></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">B</span><div class=\"opt-text\">decreased</div></div><div class=\"opt-item correct-opt\"><span class=\"opt-letter\">C</span><div class=\"opt-text\">increased</div><span class=\"corr-tag\">✓ Correct</span></div><div class=\"opt-item \"><span class=\"opt-letter\">D</span><div class=\"opt-text\">None of the above</div></div></div>\n      <details class=\"expl-box\">\n        <summary>View Explanation & Provenance (Answer: Option C)</summary>\n        <div class=\"expl-body\">\n          <p>Gravitational potential energy is stored between any two objects. So, for every object on or near Earth, there is gravitational potential energy stored between the object and Earth.\nThe amount of gravitational potential energy stored between an object and Earth depends on the mass of the object. The amount of gravitational potential energy also depends on the distance between the object and the center of Earth. This distance increases when the object moves higher and decreases when the object moves lower.\nIf the distance between an object and the center of Earth changes, the gravitational potential energy stored between the object and Earth will change. The table below shows how this change can happen.\nWhen an object&#x27;s mass stays the same and its distance from the center of Earth... | Gravitational potential energy stored between the object and Earth...\nincreases | increases\ndecreases | decreases\nstays the same | stays the same Think about how the distance between Alec and the center of Earth changed.\nThe summit of the mountain was higher than the point where Alec started hiking. As he hiked toward the summit, the distance between Alec and the center of Earth increased. So, the gravitational potential energy stored between Alec and Earth increased as he hiked toward the summit.</p>\n          \n          <div class=\"hash-row\">\n            <span><strong>Source/License:</strong> ScienceQA Open Figure Benchmark</span>\n            <span><strong>SHA-256:</strong> <code>3cf47b95fc6026e60e41...</code></span>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </details>\n    </article>\n    "}
